{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMNJWBaUrofTvqw1Z2DdSE","type":"tool_result","content":"CHANGELOG.md:12:[Omitted long matching line]\nCHANGELOG.md:17:- **Renamed `nillify_not_loaded` to `nilify_not_loaded` in `LiveVue.Encoder`**. It was a typo and the new spelling is consistent with rest of the Elixir ecosystem.\nCHANGELOG.md:36:- **AGENTS.md integration** - Installation appends LiveVue usage rules for AI-assisted development\nCHANGELOG.md:47:- Fixed installer skipping html_helpers modification for LiveVue-prefixed projects\nCHANGELOG.md:78:- Added installation instructions about LiveVue VS code extension to README.md [#86](https://github.com/Valian/live_vue/pull/86)\nCHANGELOG.md:103:- added `usage-rules.md` to help with AI-assisted development using LiveVue\nCHANGELOG.md:186:[Omitted long matching line]\nCHANGELOG.md:191:*   **`$live` Template Property**: The LiveView hook instance is now automatically available in all Vue templates as the `$live` property, providing a convenient alternative to `useLiveVue()`. The property is now also fully typed, providing autocompletion and type checking in your editor. ([#56](https://github.com/Valian/live_vue/pull/56)).\nCHANGELOG.md:193:*   **Testing Utilities**: The new `LiveVue.Test` module provides helpers to inspect Vue component configuration (props, slots, event handlers) within your LiveView tests, making it easier to write assertions. ([#46](https://github.com/Valian/live_vue/pull/46))\nCHANGELOG.md:208:    import { createLiveVue, findComponent, type LiveHook, type ComponentMap } from \"live_vue\"\nCHANGELOG.md:217:    export default createLiveVue({\nCHANGELOG.md:272:- Fix the `useLiveVue` hook typings to show all available functions and properties.\nCHANGELOG.md:331:- Added `createLiveVue` entrypoint to make it easier to customize Vue app initialization\nCHANGELOG.md:336:- `assets/vue/index.js` should export app created by `createLiveVue()`, not just available components. See migration below.\nCHANGELOG.md:355:import { createLiveVue, findComponent } from \"live_vue\"\nCHANGELOG.md:357:export default createLiveVue({\nCHANGELOG.md:394:If you had any custom initialization code, you have to move it to `createLiveVue().setup()` function.\nCHANGELOG.md:400:- Previously `initializeVueApp` was not working in SSR mode, since it was declared in app.js which couldn't be imported by server bundle. Now it's in a separate file as `createLiveVue().setup()` and can be imported by both client and server bundles.\nCHANGELOG.md:427:-   Changed `useLiveVue` inject key from `Symbol()` to `_live_vue` string, so it's working if Vite does a reload and Symbol is re-evaluated.\nguides/comparison.md:1:# LiveVue vs Alternatives\nguides/comparison.md:3:This guide compares LiveVue with other Phoenix LiveView frontend integration libraries to help you choose the right solution for your project.\nguides/comparison.md:7:LiveVue is part of a growing ecosystem of libraries that integrate modern frontend frameworks with Phoenix LiveView. Each library takes a different approach to solving the challenge of combining server-side state management with rich client-side interactivity.\nguides/comparison.md:11:| Feature | LiveVue | LiveSvelte | LiveReact | LiveState |\nguides/comparison.md:186:## LiveVue\nguides/comparison.md:188:LiveVue provides seamless Vue.js integration with Phoenix LiveView.\nguides/comparison.md:225:### When to Choose LiveVue\nguides/comparison.md:235:### Choose **LiveVue** if:\nguides/comparison.md:288:- **LiveVue**: Medium (Vue runtime)\nguides/comparison.md:307:### LiveVue\nguides/comparison.md:328:- **LiveVue** offers excellent Vue.js integration with comprehensive features\nguides/comparison.md:337:- [LiveVue Documentation](https://hexdocs.pm/live_vue)\nguides/configuration.md:3:This guide covers all configuration options available in LiveVue, from basic setup to advanced customization.\nguides/configuration.md:7:LiveVue configuration is managed in your `config/config.exs` file:\nguides/configuration.md:14:  # For development: LiveVue.SSR.ViteJS\nguides/configuration.md:15:  # For production: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS\nguides/configuration.md:48:  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.ViteJS,\nguides/configuration.md:58:  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS,\nguides/configuration.md:69:Configure your Vue application in `assets/vue/index.js`. You should use createLiveVue to provide two required functions:\nguides/configuration.md:76:Installation step provides a reasonable implementation of createLiveVue that you can use as a starting point.\nguides/configuration.md:83:import { createLiveVue, findComponent } from \"live_vue\"\nguides/configuration.md:85:export default createLiveVue({\nguides/configuration.md:121:| `plugin` | `Plugin` | LiveVue plugin (required) |\nguides/configuration.md:177:export default createLiveVue({\nguides/configuration.md:181:    // LiveVue plugin (required)\nguides/configuration.md:222:      use LiveVue.Components, vue_root: [\nguides/configuration.md:251:LiveVue provides flexible SSR options that work great in both development and production environments.\nguides/configuration.md:255:LiveVue offers two SSR strategies depending on your environment:\nguides/configuration.md:263:  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.ViteJS,\nguides/configuration.md:275:  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS,\nguides/configuration.md:324:LiveVue provides testing-specific configuration options to help with component testing and debugging.\nguides/configuration.md:328:By default, LiveVue optimizes performance by only sending prop changes (diffs) to the client. However, during testing, you may need access to the complete props state rather than just the incremental changes.\nguides/configuration.md:337:- LiveVue will always send full props and not send diffs\nguides/configuration.md:338:- The `props` field returned by `LiveVue.Test.get_vue/2` will contain the complete props state\nguides/configuration.md:360:  {NodeJS.Supervisor, [path: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS.server_path(), pool_size: 4]},\nguides/configuration.md:412:1. **Components not found**: Check `vue_root` paths in `LiveVue.Components`\nguides/installation.md:3:LiveVue replaces `esbuild` with [Vite](https://vitejs.dev/) for both client side code and SSR to achieve an amazing development experience.\nguides/installation.md:32:Create a new Phoenix project with LiveVue pre-installed:\nguides/installation.md:40:- Install and configure LiveVue automatically\nguides/installation.md:46:To add LiveVue to an existing Phoenix 1.8+ project:\nguides/installation.md:52:This will automatically configure your project with all necessary LiveVue setup.\nguides/installation.md:57:> - **Pre-Igniter LiveVue upgrade not supported**: If you have an existing LiveVue installation from before the Igniter installer was introduced, upgrading via `mix igniter.install live_vue` is not supported due to significant changes in the installation process. On the other hand, you should be able to bump version of LiveVue in your `mix.exs` file and everything should still work.\nguides/installation.md:58:> - **LiveVue itself works with Phoenix 1.7**: While the automated installer requires Phoenix 1.8+, the LiveVue library itself is compatible with Phoenix 1.7 if installed manually\nguides/installation.md:80:Now that you have LiveVue installed, check out our [Getting Started Guide](getting_started.md) to create your first Vue component!\nguides/deployment.md:3:Deploying a LiveVue app is similar to deploying a regular Phoenix app, with one key requirement: **Node.js version 19 or later must be installed** in your production environment.\nguides/deployment.md:155:- Consider contributing to [LiveVue](https://github.com/Valian/live_vue)\nguides/faq.md:5:### Why LiveVue?\nguides/faq.md:9:LiveVue allows you to create hybrid apps where:\nguides/faq.md:23:Yes, \"LiveVue\" sounds exactly like \"LiveView\" - we noticed slightly too late to change! Some helpful Reddit users pointed it out 😉\nguides/faq.md:25:We suggest referring to it as \"LiveVuejs\" in speech to avoid confusion.\nguides/faq.md:29:### How Does LiveVue Work?\nguides/faq.md:39:2. **Initialization**: The [LiveVue hook](https://github.com/Valian/live_vue/blob/main/assets/hooks.ts):\nguides/faq.md:42:   - Injects the hook for `useLiveVue`\nguides/faq.md:57:### What Optimizations Does LiveVue Use?\nguides/faq.md:59:LiveVue implements several performance optimizations:\nguides/faq.md:72:   - Uses `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol to convert structs to maps\nguides/faq.md:80:### What is the LiveVue.Encoder Protocol?\nguides/faq.md:82:The `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol is a crucial part of LiveVue's architecture that safely converts Elixir structs to maps before JSON serialization. It serves several important purposes:\nguides/faq.md:92:  @derive LiveVue.Encoder\nguides/faq.md:101:The protocol is similar to `Jason.Encoder` but converts structs to maps instead of JSON strings, which allows LiveVue to calculate minimal diffs and send only changed data over WebSocket connections.\nguides/faq.md:118:### Can I nest LiveVue components inside each other?\nguides/faq.md:126:Since the nested component's HTML is just inert markup at that point, Phoenix LiveView's hooks (including the `VueHook` that powers LiveVue) cannot be attached to it, and the nested Vue component will never be initialized.\nguides/faq.md:143:2.  **Standard Vue Components:** You can nest standard (non-LiveVue) Vue components inside a LiveVue component. These child components are defined entirely within the parent's Vue template and do not have a corresponding `<.vue>` tag in LiveView. They can receive props from their LiveVue parent and manage their own state as usual.\nguides/faq.md:146:    <!-- Parent: MyLiveVueComponent.vue -->\nguides/faq.md:163:LiveVue provides full TypeScript support out of the box. The Igniter installer sets up TypeScript automatically with proper configuration for:\nguides/faq.md:166:- LiveVue composables with full type inference\nguides/faq.md:183:Vue files in LiveVue are similar to HEEX templates. You have two main options:\nguides/faq.md:199:### How Does LiveVue Compare to LiveSvelte?\nguides/faq.md:231:- [LiveVue Examples](https://livevue.skalecki.dev) - Live demo website with interactive examples\nguides/architecture.md:1:# How LiveVue Works\nguides/architecture.md:3:This guide explains the architecture and inner workings of LiveVue, helping you understand the design decisions and implementation details.\nguides/architecture.md:11:LiveVue bridges two different paradigms: **Phoenix LiveView** with its server-side state management and HTML over WebSockets, and **Vue.js** with its client-side reactivity and virtual DOM. The challenge is making these two systems work together seamlessly while maintaining the benefits of both.\nguides/architecture.md:15:![LiveVue flow](./images/lifecycle.png)\nguides/architecture.md:21:When a LiveView renders a Vue component, LiveVue generates a special `div` element with component configuration stored in data attributes. Here's what the server generates:\nguides/architecture.md:51:export const getVueHook = ({ resolve, setup }: LiveVueApp): LiveHookInternal => ({\nguides/architecture.md:87:LiveVue implements an efficient diffing system that minimizes data transmission by sending only changed properties as JSON patches. Here's how it works:\nguides/architecture.md:93:Before diffing can occur, any custom structs are converted to maps using the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol. This ensures consistent data structures that can be reliably compared and serialized.\nguides/architecture.md:130:The combination of server-side diff calculation and client-side patch application ensures that LiveVue can handle complex, nested data structures efficiently while maintaining real-time reactivity.\nguides/architecture.md:155:#### Struct Encoding with LiveVue.Encoder\nguides/architecture.md:157:Before props are serialized and sent to the client, custom structs must be encoded using the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol. This protocol:\nguides/architecture.md:167:  @derive {LiveVue.Encoder, except: [:password]}\nguides/architecture.md:201:**Programmatic Events** use `useLiveVue().pushEvent()` for complex logic:\nguides/architecture.md:204:const live = useLiveVue()\nguides/architecture.md:224:LiveVue uses Phoenix LiveView's hook system rather than a separate JavaScript framework. This provides seamless integration within LiveView's lifecycle, automatic cleanup when elements are removed, and natural compatibility with all Phoenix events.\nguides/architecture.md:234:LiveVue minimizes data transmission by tracking only modified props, slots, and handlers. The JSON encoding is optimized to prevent redundant work, and Phoenix updates only specific data attributes rather than re-rendering entire elements.\nguides/architecture.md:238:The `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol enables efficient diffing of complex data structures:\nguides/architecture.md:249:By converting structs to consistent map representations, LiveVue can:\nguides/architecture.md:261:# LiveVue sends only the changed field\nguides/architecture.md:271:During server-side rendering, LiveVue automatically uses the Vite-generated manifest file to inject resource preload links (`<link rel=\"modulepreload\">` and others) for all the assets required by a component. This ensures that the browser can download necessary JavaScript and CSS files earlier in the page load process, improving perceived performance and reducing the time to an interactive page.\nguides/architecture.md:313:LiveVue works with standard development tools including Vue DevTools for full component inspection and debugging, Phoenix LiveView Dashboard for server-side state monitoring, and browser DevTools for network and WebSocket inspection.\nguides/architecture.md:329:### When to Use LiveVue\nguides/architecture.md:331:LiveVue is a good fit for complex client-side interactions, rich UI components with local state, leveraging the Vue ecosystem (animations, charts, etc.), and teams with Vue.js expertise.\nguides/architecture.md:337:Now that you understand how LiveVue works:\nguides/component_reference.md:67:When using `LiveVue.Components`, you can use shortcut syntax instead of the full `v-component` attribute.\nguides/component_reference.md:75:    use LiveVue.Components, vue_root: [\nguides/component_reference.md:255:LiveVue optimizes performance by sending only prop changes (diffs) instead of complete props on every update. This behavior can be controlled both globally and per-component.\nguides/component_reference.md:329:These types are pre-configured with the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol and work seamlessly with file upload functionality.\nguides/component_reference.md:331:### Custom Structs with LiveVue.Encoder\nguides/component_reference.md:333:When passing custom structs as props, you must implement the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol to ensure they are properly serialized and can be diffed efficiently. This protocol is similar to `Jason.Encoder` but converts structs to maps instead of JSON strings.\nguides/component_reference.md:342:  @derive LiveVue.Encoder\nguides/component_reference.md:348:  @derive {LiveVue.Encoder, only: [:name, :email]}\nguides/component_reference.md:354:  @derive {LiveVue.Encoder, except: [:password, :secret_key]}\nguides/component_reference.md:361:  @derive {LiveVue.Encoder, except: [:__meta__], nilify_not_loaded: true}\nguides/component_reference.md:381:defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: User do\nguides/component_reference.md:389:    |> LiveVue.Encoder.encode(opts)\nguides/component_reference.md:400:Protocol.derive(LiveVue.Encoder, SomeLibrary.User, only: [:id, :name])\nguides/component_reference.md:403:#### Why LiveVue.Encoder is Required\nguides/component_reference.md:410:4. **Optimization**: Allows LiveVue to calculate minimal diffs for prop updates\nguides/basic_usage.md:74:When passing custom structs as props, you must implement the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol:\nguides/basic_usage.md:78:  @derive LiveVue.Encoder\nguides/basic_usage.md:100:> If you get a `Protocol.UndefinedError` when passing structs as props, it means you need to implement the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol for that struct. This is a safety feature to prevent accidental exposure of sensitive data.\nguides/basic_usage.md:118:1.  **`useLiveVue()` Composable (in `<script setup>`):**\nguides/basic_usage.md:120:    Use the `useLiveVue()` composable when you need to access the hook instance for logic within your `<script setup>` block. It's ideal for pushing events programmatically.\nguides/basic_usage.md:124:    import { useLiveVue } from \"live_vue\"\nguides/basic_usage.md:127:    const live = useLiveVue()\nguides/basic_usage.md:149:    For convenience, the hook instance is also available directly in your template as the `$live` property. This is the preferred method for simple, one-off event pushes directly from the template, as it avoids the need to import and call `useLiveVue()`.\nguides/basic_usage.md:163:For navigation, LiveVue provides a built-in `Link` component that makes using `live_patch` and `live_redirect` as easy as using a standard `<a>` tag.\nguides/basic_usage.md:251:LiveVue provides seamless integration with Phoenix LiveView's file upload system through the `useLiveUpload()` composable. This handles all the complexity of managing upload state, progress tracking, and DOM elements automatically.\nguides/basic_usage.md:412:LiveVue provides full support for Phoenix LiveView's `stream()` operations. Since LiveVue already sends minimal JSON patches for all props updates, streams primarily help reduce server memory consumption by not keeping large collections in the socket assigns.\nguides/basic_usage.md:420:Note: LiveVue's automatic JSON patch diffing already ensures efficient client updates for regular props, so the main benefit of streams is server-side memory efficiency.\nguides/basic_usage.md:466:import { useLiveVue } from 'live_vue'\nguides/basic_usage.md:476:const live = useLiveVue()\nguides/basic_usage.md:513:- **Transparent Updates**: When you use `stream_insert()`, `stream_delete()`, or other stream operations, LiveVue automatically patches only the affected items\nguides/basic_usage.md:516:- **Automatic Patches**: LiveVue's existing JSON patch system handles efficient client updates\nguides/testing.md:3:LiveVue provides a robust testing module `LiveVue.Test` that makes it easy to test Vue components within your Phoenix LiveView tests.\nguides/testing.md:7:Testing LiveVue components differs from traditional Phoenix LiveView testing in a key way:\nguides/testing.md:9:- LiveVue testing provides helpers to inspect the Vue component configuration before client-side rendering\nguides/testing.md:13:The `LiveVue.Test` module requires the `lazy_html` package for parsing HTML. Add it to your test dependencies:\nguides/testing.md:27:For comprehensive testing, you should disable props diffing in your test environment to ensure `LiveVue.Test.get_vue/2` always returns complete props data:\nguides/testing.md:35:When props diffing is enabled (the default), LiveVue only sends changed properties to optimize performance. However, during testing, you typically want to inspect the complete component state rather than just the incremental changes. Disabling diffing ensures that all props are always available for testing assertions.\nguides/testing.md:46:  alias LiveVue.Test\nguides/testing.md:121:LiveVue provides tools to test both default and named slots:\nguides/testing.md:182:LiveVue's E2E tests use Playwright. Here's a typical test structure:\nguides/troubleshooting.md:3:This guide helps you diagnose and fix common issues when working with LiveVue.\nguides/troubleshooting.md:7:> New to LiveVue? Start with [Getting Started](getting_started.md) for a working example, then check [Basic Usage](basic_usage.md) for common patterns.\nguides/troubleshooting.md:92:### LiveVue.Encoder Protocol Issues\nguides/troubleshooting.md:97:- Error mentions \"LiveVue.Encoder protocol must always be explicitly implemented\"\nguides/troubleshooting.md:104:     @derive LiveVue.Encoder\nguides/troubleshooting.md:112:   Protocol.derive(LiveVue.Encoder, SomeLibrary.User, only: [:id, :name])\nguides/troubleshooting.md:119:   iex> LiveVue.Encoder.encode(user)\nguides/troubleshooting.md:137:  export function useLiveVue(): any\nguides/troubleshooting.md:138:  export function createLiveVue(config: any): any\nguides/troubleshooting.md:293:  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.ViteJS,\nguides/troubleshooting.md:311:  {NodeJS.Supervisor, [path: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS.server_path(), pool_size: 4]},\nguides/troubleshooting.md:348:import { useLiveVue } from 'live_vue'\nguides/troubleshooting.md:350:const live = useLiveVue()\nguides/troubleshooting.md:473:1. **LiveVue version**\nguides/forms.md:3:This guide covers the `useLiveForm` composable for building complex forms with server-side validation, nested objects, and dynamic arrays in LiveVue.\nguides/forms.md:7:> New to LiveVue? Check out [Basic Usage](basic_usage.md) for fundamental patterns before diving into forms.\nguides/forms.md:517:LiveVue supports three checkbox patterns that automatically handle different use cases:\nguides/forms.md:556:When you create multiple checkboxes for the same field path with different values, LiveVue automatically detects this as a multi-checkbox scenario and manages an array:\nguides/forms.md:1184:For building reusable form components, LiveVue provides `useField()` and `useArrayField()` hooks that work with Vue's provide/inject system.\nguides/forms.md:1373:LiveVue forms work seamlessly with Ecto changesets:\nguides/forms.md:1920:Now that you understand LiveVue forms, you might want to explore:\nguides/forms.md:1925:- [Basic Usage](basic_usage.md) for other LiveVue patterns and features\nguides/getting_started.md:3:Now that you have LiveVue installed, let's create your first Vue component and integrate it with LiveView.\nguides/getting_started.md:148:This example demonstrates several key LiveVue features:\nguides/getting_started.md:158:![LiveVue flow](./images/lifecycle.png)\nguides/getting_started.md:164:When you start passing more complex data structures as props, you'll need to implement the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol:\nguides/getting_started.md:169:  @derive LiveVue.Encoder\nguides/getting_started.md:192:> - In development, LiveVue enables Hot Module Replacement for instant component updates\nguides/getting_started.md:199:- [Live Examples](https://livevue.skalecki.dev) - Interactive demos to see LiveVue in action\nguides/client_api.md:3:This guide documents all client-side utilities, composables, and APIs available in LiveVue for Vue components.\nguides/client_api.md:7:> New to LiveVue? Check out [Basic Usage](basic_usage.md) for fundamental patterns before diving into the API details.\nguides/client_api.md:11:LiveVue provides several Vue Composables to make interacting with the LiveView instance from your components easier and more declarative. These should be your first choice when working inside `.vue` files.\nguides/client_api.md:13:### `useLiveVue()`\nguides/client_api.md:15:The `useLiveVue()` composable is the standard way to get access to the LiveView hook instance within your component's `<script setup>` block. This is the method you should use when you need to call hook methods from your script logic (e.g., in watchers, lifecycle hooks, or other functions).\nguides/client_api.md:19:import { useLiveVue } from 'live_vue'\nguides/client_api.md:22:const live = useLiveVue()\nguides/client_api.md:33:It is a wrapper around `useLiveVue().handleEvent()` that saves you the boilerplate of using `onMounted` and `onUnmounted`.\nguides/client_api.md:315:import { useLiveConnection, useLiveVue } from 'live_vue'\nguides/client_api.md:319:const live = useLiveVue()\nguides/client_api.md:557:#### 1. `useLiveVue()` Composable\nguides/client_api.md:559:As seen above, `useLiveVue()` returns the raw hook instance.\nguides/client_api.md:563:For convenience, the LiveView hook instance is also exposed directly to your Vue templates as a global property named `$live`. This is ideal for simple, one-off calls directly from an element's event handler, as it saves you from importing and calling `useLiveVue()` when you don't need the instance in your script.\nguides/client_api.md:574:Both `useLiveVue()` and `$live` return the same hook instance, which is fully typed and provides access to the methods below.\nguides/client_api.md:578:The hook instance (returned by `useLiveVue()` or accessed via `$live`) provides the following methods:\nguides/client_api.md:586:import { useLiveVue } from 'live_vue'\nguides/client_api.md:587:const live = useLiveVue()\nguides/client_api.md:613:import { useLiveVue } from 'live_vue'\nguides/client_api.md:615:const live = useLiveVue()","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzoENcH6Ai7NwA6972BSx3","session_id":"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d","uuid":"01fc3555-460a-4e5d-b086-673e17c1f5eb"}
