2026-02-11T03:10:56.647Z\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"5f5770ae-f400-4665-8b9f-c5e32888fc89\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01K4vfESAA425zXcRttv63Cu\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01CpDE9VWr8UdaihRftG9N6a\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue/lib/live_vue.ex\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":3,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":14854,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":14854},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"}},\"requestId\":\"req_011CY1Yn2tJUgmnJYMGC5W8G\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"27f5d343-b456-4f1e-82f2-d3a432a4fb9f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:57.158Z\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"27f5d343-b456-4f1e-82f2-d3a432a4fb9f\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"progress\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"hook_progress\",\"hookEvent\":\"PostToolUse\",\"hookName\":\"PostToolUse:Read\",\"command\":\"callback\"},\"parentToolUseID\":\"toolu_01CpDE9VWr8UdaihRftG9N6a\",\"toolUseID\":\"toolu_01CpDE9VWr8UdaihRftG9N6a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:57.162Z\",\"uuid\":\"45e57504-0e25-4ac4-bb30-1ee0b8af9d38\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"27f5d343-b456-4f1e-82f2-d3a432a4fb9f\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01CpDE9VWr8UdaihRftG9N6a\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"     1→defmodule LiveVue do\\n     2→  @moduledoc \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n     3→  LiveVue provides seamless integration between Phoenix LiveView and Vue.js components.\\n     4→\\n     5→  ## Installation and Configuration\\n     6→\\n     7→  See README.md for installation instructions and usage.\\n     8→\\n     9→  ## Component Options\\n    10→\\n    11→  When using the `vue/1` component or `~V` sigil, the following options are supported:\\n    12→\\n    13→  ### Required Attributes\\n    14→    * `v-component` (string) - Name of the Vue component (e.g., \\\"YourComponent\\\", \\\"directory/Example\\\")\\n    15→\\n    16→  > #### Tip {: .tip}\\n    17→  >\\n    18→  > Value of `v-component` will be directly passed to `resolve` function of the `createLiveVue` instance.\\n    19→  > It should return Vue component or a promise that resolves to a Vue component.\\n    20→  > In a standard setup, you can find it in `assets/vue/index.js`.\\n    21→\\n    22→  ### Optional Attributes\\n    23→    * `id` (string) - Explicit ID of the wrapper component. If not provided, a random one will be\\n    24→      generated. Useful to keep ID consistent in development (e.g., \\\"vue-1\\\")\\n    25→    * `class` (string) - CSS class(es) to apply to the Vue component wrapper\\n    26→      (e.g., \\\"my-class\\\" or \\\"my-class another-class\\\")\\n    27→    * `v-ssr` (boolean) - Whether to render the component on the server. Defaults to the value set\\n    28→      in config (default: true)\\n    29→    * `v-socket` (LiveView.Socket) - LiveView socket, should be provided when rendering inside LiveView\\n    30→\\n    31→  ### Event Handlers\\n    32→    * `v-on:*` - Vue event handlers can be attached using the `v-on:` prefix\\n    33→      (e.g., `v-on:click`, `v-on:input`)\\n    34→\\n    35→  ### Props and Slots\\n    36→    * All other attributes are passed as props to the Vue component\\n    37→    * Slots can be passed as regular Phoenix slots\\n    38→  \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    39→\\n    40→  use Phoenix.Component\\n    41→\\n    42→  import Phoenix.HTML\\n    43→\\n    44→  alias LiveVue.Encoder\\n    45→  alias LiveVue.Slots\\n    46→  alias LiveVue.SSR\\n    47→  alias Phoenix.LiveView\\n    48→  alias Phoenix.LiveView.LiveStream\\n    49→\\n    50→  require Logger\\n    51→\\n    52→  @ssr_default Application.compile_env(:live_vue, :ssr, true)\\n    53→  @diff_default Application.compile_env(:live_vue, :enable_props_diff, true)\\n    54→\\n    55→  defmacro __using__(_opts) do\\n    56→    quote do\\n    57→      import LiveVue\\n    58→    end\\n    59→  end\\n    60→\\n    61→  @doc \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    62→  Renders a Vue component within Phoenix LiveView.\\n    63→\\n    64→  ## Examples\\n    65→\\n    66→      <.vue\\n    67→        v-component=\\\"MyComponent\\\"\\n    68→        message=\\\"Hello\\\"\\n    69→        v-on:click=\\\"handleClick\\\"\\n    70→        class=\\\"my-component\\\"\\n    71→      />\\n    72→\\n    73→      <.vue\\n    74→        v-component=\\\"nested/Component\\\"\\n    75→        v-ssr={false}\\n    76→        items={@items}\\n    77→      >\\n    78→        <:default>Default slot content</:default>\\n    79→        <:named>Named slot content</:named>\\n    80→      </.vue>\\n    81→  \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    82→  def vue(assigns) do\\n    83→    init = assigns.__changed__ == nil\\n    84→    dead = assigns[:\\\"v-socket\\\"] == nil or not LiveView.connected?(assigns[:\\\"v-socket\\\"])\\n    85→    use_diff = Map.get(assigns, :\\\"v-diff\\\", @diff_default)\\n    86→    use_streams_diff = Enum.any?(assigns, fn {_k, v} -> match?(%LiveStream{}, v) end)\\n    87→    render_ssr? = init and dead and Map.get(assigns, :\\\"v-ssr\\\", @ssr_default)\\n    88→\\n    89→    # if we enable diffs, we use only changed props for all the remaining calculations\\n    90→    base_assigns =\\n    91→      if use_diff do\\n    92→        Enum.filter(assigns, fn {k, _v} -> key_changed(assigns, k) end)\\n    93→      else\\n    94→        assigns\\n    95→      end\\n    96→\\n    97→    props = extract(base_assigns, :props)\\n    98→    streams = extract(base_assigns, :streams)\\n    99→    slots = extract(base_assigns, :slots)\\n   100→    handlers = extract(base_assigns, :handlers)\\n   101→    props_diff = if use_diff, do: calculate_props_diff(props, assigns), else: []\\n   102→    streams_diff = if use_streams_diff, do: calculate_streams_diff(streams, init or dead), else: []\\n   103→\\n   104→    assigns =\\n   105→      assigns\\n   106→      |> Map.put_new(:class, nil)\\n   107→      |> Map.put(:__component_name, Map.get(assigns, :\\\"v-component\\\"))\\n   108→      |> Map.put(:props, props)\\n   109→      # let's compress it a little bit, and decompress it on the client side\\n   110→      |> Map.put(:props_diff, Enum.map(props_diff, &prepare_diff/1))\\n   111→      |> Map.put(:streams_diff, Enum.map(streams_diff, &prepare_diff/1))\\n   112→      |> Map.put(:handlers, handlers)\\n   113→      |> Map.put(:slots, Slots.rendered_slot_map(slots))\\n   114→      |> Map.put(:use_diff, use_diff)\\n   115→\\n   116→    assigns =\\n   117→      Map.put(assigns, :ssr_render, if(render_ssr?, do: ssr_render(assigns)))\\n   118→\\n   119→    computed_changed =\\n   120→      %{\\n   121→        # we send initial props only on initial render, later we send only changed props\\n   122→        props: init or dead or not use_diff,\\n   123→        ssr_render: render_ssr?,\\n   124→        slots: slots != %{},\\n   125→        handlers: handlers != %{},\\n   126→        # we want to send props_diff always but not on initial render\\n   127→        props_diff: not init and not dead and use_diff,\\n   128→        # we want to send stream_diffs always when there's at least one stream in assigns\\n   129→        streams_diff: use_streams_diff\\n   130→      }\\n   131→\\n   132→    assigns =\\n   133→      update_in(assigns.__changed__, fn\\n   134→        nil -> nil\\n   135→        changed -> for {k, true} <- computed_changed, into: changed, do: {k, true}\\n   136→      end)\\n   137→\\n   138→    # optimizing diffs by using string interpolation\\n   139→    # https://elixirforum.com/t/heex-attribute-value-in-quotes-send-less-data-than-values-in-braces/63274\\n   140→    ~H\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   141→    {raw(@ssr_render[:preloadLinks])}\\n   142→    <div\\n   143→      id={assigns[:id] || id(@__component_name)}\\n   144→      data-name={@__component_name}\\n   145→      data-props={\\\"#{json(Encoder.encode(@props))}\\\"}\\n   146→      data-props-diff={\\\"#{json(@props_diff)}\\\"}\\n   147→      data-streams-diff={\\\"#{json(@streams_diff)}\\\"}\\n   148→      data-ssr={(@ssr_render != nil) |> to_string()}\\n   149→      data-use-diff={@use_diff |> to_string()}\\n   150→      data-handlers={\\\"#{for({k, v} <- @handlers, into: %{}, do: {k, json(v.ops)}) |> json()}\\\"}\\n   151→      data-slots={\\\"#{@slots |> Slots.base_encode_64() |> json}\\\"}\\n   152→      phx-update=\\\"ignore\\\"\\n   153→      phx-hook=\\\"VueHook\\\"\\n   154→      phx-no-format\\n   155→      class={@class}\\n   156→    ><%= raw(@ssr_render[:html]) %></div>\\n   157→    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   158→  end\\n   159→\\n   160→  # Calculates minimal JSON Patch operations for changed props only.\\n   161→  # Uses Phoenix LiveView's __changed__ tracking to identify what props have changed.\\n   162→  # For simple values, generates direct replace operations.\\n   163→  # For complex values (maps, lists), uses Jsonpatch.diff to find minimal changes.\\n   164→  # Uses LiveVue.Encoder to safely encode structs before diffing.\\n   165→  defp calculate_props_diff(props, %{__changed__: changed}) do\\n   166→    # For simple types: changed[k] == true\\n   167→    # For complex types: changed[k] is the old value\\n   168→    props\\n   169→    |> Enum.flat_map(fn {k, new_value} ->\\n   170→      case changed[k] do\\n   171→        nil ->\\n   172→          []\\n   173→\\n   174→        # For simple types, generate replace operation\\n   175→        true ->\\n   176→          [%{op: \\\"replace\\\", path: \\\"/#{k}\\\", value: Encoder.encode(new_value)}]\\n   177→\\n   178→        # For complex types which didn't change type, use Jsonpatch to find minimal diff\\n   179→        old_value ->\\n   180→          Jsonpatch.diff(old_value, new_value,\\n   181→            ancestor_path: \\\"/#{k}\\\",\\n   182→            prepare_map: fn\\n   183→              struct when is_struct(struct) -> Encoder.encode(struct)\\n   184→              rest -> rest\\n   185→            end,\\n   186→            object_hash: &object_hash/1\\n   187→          )\\n   188→      end\\n   189→    end)\\n   190→    # let's add a harmless test operation to ensure we never have the same diff as before\\n   191→    |> then(fn diff -> [%{op: \\\"test\\\", path: \\\"\\\", value: :rand.uniform(10_000_000)} | diff] end)\\n   192→  end\\n   193→\\n   194→  # Generates JSON patch operations for LiveStream changes\\n   195→  # Handles insertions and deletions for Phoenix LiveView streams\\n   196→  defp calculate_streams_diff(streams, initial)\\n   197→\\n   198→  defp calculate_streams_diff(streams, true) do\\n   199→    # for initial render, we want to reset all streams, and then apply the diffs\\n   200→    init = Enum.map(streams, fn {k, _} -> %{op: \\\"replace\\\", path: \\\"/#{k}\\\", value: []} end)\\n   201→    diffs = Enum.flat_map(streams, fn {k, stream} -> generate_stream_patches(k, stream) end)\\n   202→    init ++ diffs\\n   203→  end\\n   204→\\n   205→  defp calculate_streams_diff(streams, false) do\\n   206→    streams\\n   207→    |> Enum.flat_map(fn {k, stream} -> generate_stream_patches(k, stream) end)\\n   208→    |> then(fn diff -> [%{op: \\\"test\\\", path: \\\"\\\", value: :rand.uniform(10_000_000)} | diff] end)\\n   209→  end\\n   210→\\n   211→  # this function tells which field of the struct should be used as a key for the diff\\n   212→  # right now it's hardcoded as id, maybe in the future I'll add a way to configure it,\\n   213→  # depending on the user's needs\\n   214→  defp object_hash(%{id: id}), do: id\\n   215→  defp object_hash(_), do: nil\\n   216→\\n   217→  # Generates JSON patch operations for LiveStream changes\\n   218→  # Handles insertions and deletions for Phoenix LiveView streams\\n   219→  defp generate_stream_patches(stream_name, %LiveStream{} = stream) do\\n   220→    patches = []\\n   221→\\n   222→    # Handle reset operation first\\n   223→    patches =\\n   224→      if stream.reset?,\\n   225→        do: [%{op: \\\"replace\\\", path: \\\"/#{stream_name}\\\", value: []} | patches],\\n   226→        else: patches\\n   227→\\n   228→    # Handle deletions second\\n   229→    patches =\\n   230→      Enum.reduce(stream.deletes, patches, fn dom_id, patches ->\\n   231→        [%{op: \\\"remove\\\", path: \\\"/#{stream_name}/$$#{dom_id}\\\"} | patches]\\n   232→      end)\\n   233→\\n   234→    # Handle insertions third, but reversed - inserts at -1 should be correctly ordered, inserts at 0 should be reversed\\n   235→    # see https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix_live_view/Phoenix.LiveView.html#stream/4 :at option\\n   236→    stream.inserts\\n   237→    |> Enum.reverse()\\n   238→    |> Enum.reduce(patches, fn {dom_id, at, item, limit, update_only}, patches ->\\n   239→      item = Map.put(Encoder.encode(item), :__dom_id, dom_id)\\n   240→\\n   241→      patches =\\n   242→        if update_only,\\n   243→          do: [%{op: \\\"replace\\\", path: \\\"/#{stream_name}/$$#{dom_id}\\\", value: item} | patches],\\n   244→          else: [%{op: \\\"upsert\\\", path: \\\"/#{stream_name}/#{if at == -1, do: \\\"-\\\", else: at}\\\", value: item} | patches]\\n   245→\\n   246→      if limit,\\n   247→        do: [%{op: \\\"limit\\\", path: \\\"/#{stream_name}\\\", value: limit} | patches],\\n   248→        else: patches\\n   249→    end)\\n   250→    |> Enum.reverse()\\n   251→  end\\n   252→\\n   253→  # we compress the diff to make it smaller, so it's faster to send to the client\\n   254→  # then, it's decompressed on the client side\\n   255→  defp prepare_diff(%{op: op, path: p, value: value}), do: [op, p, value]\\n   256→  defp prepare_diff(%{op: op, path: p}), do: [op, p]\\n   257→\\n   258→  defp extract(assigns, type) do\\n   259→    Enum.reduce(assigns, %{}, fn {key, value}, acc ->\\n   260→      case normalize_key(key, value) do\\n   261→        ^type -> Map.put(acc, key, value)\\n   262→        {^type, k} -> Map.put(acc, k, value)\\n   263→        _ -> acc\\n   264→      end\\n   265→    end)\\n   266→  end\\n   267→\\n   268→  defp normalize_key(key, _val) when key in ~w\\\"id class v-ssr v-diff v-component v-socket __changed__ __given__\\\"a,\\n   269→    do: :special\\n   270→\\n   271→  defp normalize_key(_key, [%{__slot__: _}]), do: :slots\\n   272→  defp normalize_key(key, val) when is_atom(key), do: key |> to_string() |> normalize_key(val)\\n   273→  defp normalize_key(\\\"v-on:\\\" <> key, _val), do: {:handlers, key}\\n   274→  defp normalize_key(_key, %LiveStream{}), do: :streams\\n   275→  defp normalize_key(_key, _val), do: :props\\n   276→\\n   277→  defp key_changed(%{__changed__: nil}, _key), do: true\\n   278→  defp key_changed(%{__changed__: changed}, key), do: changed[key] != nil\\n   279→\\n   280→  defp ssr_render(assigns) do\\n   281→    name = assigns[:\\\"v-component\\\"]\\n   282→    encoded_props = Encoder.encode(assigns.props)\\n   283→\\n   284→    case SSR.render(name, encoded_props, assigns.slots) do\\n   285→      {:error, message} ->\\n   286→        Logger.error(\\\"Vue SSR error: #{message}\\\")\\n   287→        nil\\n   288→\\n   289→      %{preloadLinks: links, html: html} ->\\n   290→        %{preloadLinks: links, html: html}\\n   291→    end\\n   292→  rescue\\n   293→    SSR.NotConfigured ->\\n   294→      nil\\n   295→  end\\n   296→\\n   297→  defp json(data), do: Jason.encode!(data, escape: :html_safe)\\n   298→\\n   299→  defp id(name) do\\n   300→    # a small trick to avoid collisions of IDs but keep them consistent across dead and live render\\n   301→    # id(name) is called only once during the whole LiveView lifecycle because it's not using any assigns\\n   302→    number = Process.get(:live_vue_counter, 1)\\n   303→    Process.put(:live_vue_counter, number + 1)\\n   304→    \\\"#{name}-#{number}\\\"\\n   305→  end\\n   306→\\n   307→  @doc false\\n   308→  def get_socket(assigns) do\\n   309→    case get_in(assigns, [:vue_opts, :socket]) || assigns[:socket] do\\n   310→      %LiveView.Socket{} = socket -> socket\\n   311→      _ -> nil\\n   312→    end\\n   313→  end\\n   314→\\n   315→  @doc false\\n   316→  @deprecated \\\"~V sigil is deprecated, please use ~VUE instead.\\\"\\n   317→  defmacro sigil_V(term, modifiers) do\\n   318→    do_sigil(term, modifiers, __CALLER__)\\n   319→  end\\n   320→\\n   321→  @doc \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   322→  Inlines a Vue single-file component inside a LiveView. This is the new recommended way over the `~V` sigil.\\n   323→  \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   324→  defmacro sigil_VUE(term, modifiers) do\\n   325→    do_sigil(term, modifiers, __CALLER__)\\n   326→  end\\n   327→\\n   328→  defp do_sigil({:<<>>, _meta, [string]}, [], caller) do\\n   329→    path = \\\"./assets/vue/_build/#{caller.module}.vue\\\"\\n   330→\\n   331→    with :ok <- File.mkdir_p(Path.dirname(path)) do\\n   332→      File.write!(path, string)\\n   333→    end\\n   334→\\n   335→    quote do\\n   336→      ~H\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   337→      <LiveVue.vue\\n   338→        class={get_in(assigns, [:vue_opts, :class])}\\n   339→        v-component={\\\"_build/#{__MODULE__}\\\"}\\n   340→        v-socket={get_socket(assigns)}\\n   341→        v-ssr={get_in(assigns, [:vue_opts, :ssr]) != false}\\n   342→        {Map.drop(assigns, [:vue_opts, :socket, :flash, :live_action])}\\n   343→      />\\n   344→      \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   345→    end\\n   346→  end\\n   347→end\\n   348→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"8b9007b3-647c-412a-b2de-8a6d58cc1e20\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:57.162Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"27f5d343-b456-4f1e-82f2-d3a432a4fb9f\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"8b9007b3-647c-412a-b2de-8a6d58cc1e20\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01K4vfESAA425zXcRttv63Cu\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01RCEKx3D4X8VWoLxTgoF2a3\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue/lib/live_vue/components.ex\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":3,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":14854,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":14854},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"}},\"requestId\":\"req_011CY1Yn2tJUgmnJYMGC5W8G\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"52dd15ec-39d9-4a5a-9adf-b2e5743df681\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:57.609Z\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"52dd15ec-39d9-4a5a-9adf-b2e5743df681\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"progress\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"hook_progress\",\"hookEvent\":\"PostToolUse\",\"hookName\":\"PostToolUse:Read\",\"command\":\"callback\"},\"parentToolUseID\":\"toolu_01RCEKx3D4X8VWoLxTgoF2a3\",\"toolUseID\":\"toolu_01RCEKx3D4X8VWoLxTgoF2a3\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:57.612Z\",\"uuid\":\"028acbbe-6f24-4207-ab68-31dd97148701\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"52dd15ec-39d9-4a5a-9adf-b2e5743df681\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01RCEKx3D4X8VWoLxTgoF2a3\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"     1→defmodule LiveVue.Components do\\n     2→  @moduledoc \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n     3→  Macros to improve the developer experience of crossing the Liveview/Vue boundary.\\n     4→  \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n     5→\\n     6→  @doc \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n     7→  Generates functions local to your current module that can be used to render Vue components.\\n     8→  TODO: This could perhaps be optimized to only read the files once per compilation.\\n     9→\\n    10→  ## Examples\\n    11→\\n    12→  ```elixir\\n    13→  use LiveVue.Components, vue_root: [\\\"./assets/vue\\\", \\\"./lib/my_app_web\\\"]\\n    14→  ```\\n    15→  \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    16→  defmacro __using__(opts) do\\n    17→    opts\\n    18→    |> Keyword.get(:vue_root, [\\\"./assets/vue\\\"])\\n    19→    |> List.wrap()\\n    20→    |> Enum.flat_map(fn vue_root ->\\n    21→      if String.contains?(vue_root, \\\"*\\\"),\\n    22→        do:\\n    23→          raise(\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    24→          Glob pattern is not supported in :vue_root, please specify a list of directories.\\n    25→\\n    26→          Example:\\n    27→\\n    28→          use LiveVue.Components, vue_root: [\\\"./assets/vue\\\", \\\"./lib/my_app_web\\\"]\\n    29→          \\\"\\\"\\\")\\n    30→\\n    31→      vue_root\\n    32→      |> Path.join(\\\"**/*.vue\\\")\\n    33→      |> Path.wildcard()\\n    34→      |> Enum.map(&Path.basename(&1, \\\".vue\\\"))\\n    35→    end)\\n    36→    |> Enum.uniq()\\n    37→    |> Enum.map(&name_to_function/1)\\n    38→  end\\n    39→\\n    40→  defp name_to_function(name) do\\n    41→    quote do\\n    42→      def unquote(:\\\"#{name}\\\")(assigns) do\\n    43→        assigns\\n    44→        |> Map.put(:\\\"v-component\\\", unquote(name))\\n    45→        |> LiveVue.vue()\\n    46→      end\\n    47→    end\\n    48→  end\\n    49→end\\n    50→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"b9127ed2-efb2-4741-a2d4-6f4cad869e50\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:57.612Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"52dd15ec-39d9-4a5a-9adf-b2e5743df681\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"b9127ed2-efb2-4741-a2d4-6f4cad869e50\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01K4vfESAA425zXcRttv63Cu\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_0147in5FdB1Lb7wbp3Pdm7ev\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue/lib/live_vue/encoder.ex\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":3,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":14854,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":14854},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"}},\"requestId\":\"req_011CY1Yn2tJUgmnJYMGC5W8G\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"cc5e722b-015f-452f-a350-f58720bfbaa8\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:58.053Z\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"cc5e722b-015f-452f-a350-f58720bfbaa8\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"progress\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"hook_progress\",\"hookEvent\":\"PostToolUse\",\"hookName\":\"PostToolUse:Read\",\"command\":\"callback\"},\"parentToolUseID\":\"toolu_0147in5FdB1Lb7wbp3Pdm7ev\",\"toolUseID\":\"toolu_0147in5FdB1Lb7wbp3Pdm7ev\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:58.055Z\",\"uuid\":\"83b19aa3-90c1-4a08-a333-2880f7e62a99\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"cc5e722b-015f-452f-a350-f58720bfbaa8\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_0147in5FdB1Lb7wbp3Pdm7ev\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"     1→defprotocol LiveVue.Encoder do\\n     2→  @moduledoc \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n     3→  Protocol for encoding values to JSON for LiveVue.\\n     4→\\n     5→  This protocol is used to safely transform structs into plain maps before\\n     6→  calculating JSON patches. It ensures that struct fields are explicitly\\n     7→  exposed and prevents accidental exposure of sensitive data.\\n     8→\\n     9→  It's very similar to Jason.Encoder, but it's converting structs to maps instead of strings.\\n    10→\\n    11→  ## Deriving\\n    12→\\n    13→  The protocol allows leveraging Elixir's `@derive` feature to simplify protocol\\n    14→  implementation in trivial cases. Accepted options are:\\n    15→\\n    16→  * `:only` - encodes only values of specified keys.\\n    17→  * `:except` - encodes all struct fields except specified keys.\\n    18→\\n    19→  By default all keys except the `:__struct__` key are encoded.\\n    20→\\n    21→  ## Example\\n    22→\\n    23→  Let's assume a presence of the following struct:\\n    24→\\n    25→      defmodule User do\\n    26→        defstruct [:name, :email, :password]\\n    27→      end\\n    28→\\n    29→  If we were to call `@derive LiveVue.Encoder` just before `defstruct`, an\\n    30→  implementation would be generated that encodes all fields except `:__struct__`:\\n    31→\\n    32→      defmodule User do\\n    33→        @derive LiveVue.Encoder\\n    34→        defstruct [:name, :email, :password]\\n    35→      end\\n    36→\\n    37→  If we called `@derive {LiveVue.Encoder, only: [:name, :email]}`, only the\\n    38→  specified fields would be encoded:\\n    39→\\n    40→      defmodule User do\\n    41→        @derive {LiveVue.Encoder, only: [:name, :email]}\\n    42→        defstruct [:name, :email, :password]\\n    43→      end\\n    44→\\n    45→  If we called `@derive {LiveVue.Encoder, except: [:password]}`, all fields\\n    46→  except the specified ones would be encoded:\\n    47→\\n    48→      defmodule User do\\n    49→        @derive {LiveVue.Encoder, except: [:password]}\\n    50→        defstruct [:name, :email, :password]\\n    51→      end\\n    52→\\n    53→  ## Deriving outside of the module\\n    54→\\n    55→  If you don't own the struct you want to encode, you may use Protocol.derive/3 placed outside of any module:\\n    56→\\n    57→  Protocol.derive(LiveVue.Encoder, User, only: [...])\\n    58→\\n    59→  ## Custom implementations\\n    60→\\n    61→  You may define your own implementation for the struct:\\n    62→\\n    63→  defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: User do\\n    64→    def encode(struct, opts) do\\n    65→      struct\\n    66→      |> Map.take([:first, :second])\\n    67→      |> LiveVue.Encoder.encode(opts)\\n    68→    end\\n    69→  end\\n    70→  \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    71→\\n    72→  @type t :: term\\n    73→  @type opts :: Keyword.t()\\n    74→  @fallback_to_any true\\n    75→\\n    76→  @doc \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    77→  Encodes a value to one of the primitive types.\\n    78→  \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    79→  @spec encode(t, opts) :: any()\\n    80→  def encode(value, opts \\\\\\\\ [])\\n    81→end\\n    82→\\n    83→# Primitive types that are already JSON serializable\\n    84→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Integer do\\n    85→  def encode(value, _opts), do: value\\n    86→end\\n    87→\\n    88→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Float do\\n    89→  def encode(value, _opts), do: value\\n    90→end\\n    91→\\n    92→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: BitString do\\n    93→  def encode(value, _opts), do: value\\n    94→end\\n    95→\\n    96→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Atom do\\n    97→  def encode(atom, _opts), do: atom\\n    98→end\\n    99→\\n   100→# Complex types that need recursive encoding\\n   101→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: List do\\n   102→  def encode(list, opts) do\\n   103→    Enum.map(list, &LiveVue.Encoder.encode(&1, opts))\\n   104→  end\\n   105→end\\n   106→\\n   107→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Map do\\n   108→  def encode(map, opts) do\\n   109→    Map.new(map, fn {key, value} ->\\n   110→      {key, LiveVue.Encoder.encode(value, opts)}\\n   111→    end)\\n   112→  end\\n   113→end\\n   114→\\n   115→# Date and time types - Jason encoder handles them\\n   116→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: [Date, Time, NaiveDateTime, DateTime] do\\n   117→  def encode(value, _opts) do\\n   118→    # otherwise diff is comparing tuples, and that's not what we want\\n   119→    @for.to_iso8601(value)\\n   120→  end\\n   121→end\\n   122→\\n   123→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Phoenix.HTML.Form do\\n   124→  def encode(%Phoenix.HTML.Form{} = form, opts) do\\n   125→    LiveVue.Encoder.encode(\\n   126→      %{\\n   127→        name: form.name,\\n   128→        values: encode_form_values(form, opts),\\n   129→        errors: encode_form_errors(form) || %{},\\n   130→        valid: get_form_validity(form)\\n   131→      },\\n   132→      opts\\n   133→    )\\n   134→  rescue\\n   135→    error in [Protocol.UndefinedError] ->\\n   136→      reraise maybe_enhance_error(error), __STACKTRACE__\\n   137→  end\\n   138→\\n   139→  defp get_form_validity(%{source: %{valid?: valid}}), do: valid\\n   140→  defp get_form_validity(_), do: true\\n   141→\\n   142→  # Conditionally compiled helper to enhance error messages when Ecto is available\\n   143→  if Code.ensure_loaded?(Ecto) do\\n   144→    defp maybe_enhance_error(%{value: %Ecto.Association.NotLoaded{}} = error) do\\n   145→      Map.update!(error, :description, fn description ->\\n   146→        [first | rest] = String.split(description, \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\")\\n   147→\\n   148→        addition = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   149→        To prevent this error from happening in forms, you can explicitly encode form using LiveVue.Encoder.encode(form, nilify_not_loaded: true) option.\\n   150→        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   151→\\n   152→        Enum.join([first | [addition | rest]], \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\")\\n   153→      end)\\n   154→    end\\n   155→\\n   156→    defp maybe_enhance_error(error), do: error\\n   157→  else\\n   158→    defp maybe_enhance_error(error), do: error\\n   159→  end\\n   160→\\n   161→  if Code.ensure_loaded?(Ecto) do\\n   162→    @relations [:embed, :assoc]\\n   163→\\n   164→    # for changeset, we need to collect actual values from the changeset\\n   165→    # - if there are changes, we need to use changed values\\n   166→    # - if there are no changes, we should use params (possibly invalid, to reflect intermediate state)\\n   167→    # - if there are not changes and no params, we should use the data\\n   168→    # it's a bit tricky, because we need to traverse embeds and assocs\\n   169→    defp collect_changeset_values(%Ecto.Changeset{} = source, opts) do\\n   170→      data = Map.new(source.types, fn {field, type} -> {field, get_field_value(source, field, type, opts)} end)\\n   171→\\n   172→      result = if is_struct(source.data), do: Map.merge(source.data, data), else: data\\n   173→\\n   174→      # Filter out __meta__ field from Ecto schemas\\n   175→      Map.delete(result, :__meta__)\\n   176→    end\\n   177→\\n   178→    defp get_field_value(source, field, {tag, %{cardinality: :one}}, opts) when tag in @relations do\\n   179→      case Map.fetch(source.changes, field) do\\n   180→        {:ok, nil} ->\\n   181→          nil\\n   182→\\n   183→        {:ok, %Ecto.Changeset{} = changeset} ->\\n   184→          collect_changeset_values(changeset, opts)\\n   185→\\n   186→        # there are no changes underneath, so we can just use the data\\n   187→        :error ->\\n   188→          case Map.fetch!(source.data, field) do\\n   189→            %Ecto.Association.NotLoaded{} = not_loaded ->\\n   190→              # I was thinking how to solve this correctly\\n   191→              # but for now I think ignoring this is the best option\\n   192→              if opts[:nilify_not_loaded], do: nil, else: not_loaded\\n   193→\\n   194→            %{__meta__: _} = value ->\\n   195→              Map.delete(value, :__meta__)\\n   196→\\n   197→            value ->\\n   198→              value\\n   199→          end\\n   200→      end\\n   201→    end\\n   202→\\n   203→    defp get_field_value(source, field, {tag, %{cardinality: :many}}, opts) when tag in @relations do\\n   204→      case Map.fetch(source.changes, field) do\\n   205→        {:ok, changesets} ->\\n   206→          changesets\\n   207→          # we need to filter out changesets that have no params - they are removed from the form\\n   208→          |> Enum.filter(&(&1.params != nil))\\n   209→          |> Enum.map(&collect_changeset_values(&1, opts))\\n   210→\\n   211→        :error ->\\n   212→          case Map.fetch!(source.data, field) do\\n   213→            %Ecto.Association.NotLoaded{} = not_loaded ->\\n   214→              # let's return nil for not loaded associations\\n   215→              if opts[:nilify_not_loaded], do: nil, else: not_loaded\\n   216→\\n   217→            [%{__meta__: _} | _] = value ->\\n   218→              Enum.map(value, &Map.delete(&1, :__meta__))\\n   219→\\n   220→            value ->\\n   221→              value\\n   222→          end\\n   223→      end\\n   224→    end\\n   225→\\n   226→    defp get_field_value(source, field, _type, _opts) do\\n   227→      Phoenix.HTML.FormData.Ecto.Changeset.input_value(source, %{params: source.params}, field)\\n   228→\\n   229→      # # First try to get the value using the standard Phoenix input_value function\\n   230→      # case Phoenix.HTML.FormData.Ecto.Changeset.input_value(source, %{params: source.params}, field) do\\n   231→      #   nil ->\\n   232→      #     # If that returns nil, check if there's a field with [] suffix in params\\n   233→      #     # This handles the case where multiple checkbox fields have names like \\\"preferences[]\\\"\\n   234→      #     field_with_brackets = \\\"#{field}[]\\\"\\n   235→      #     case Map.get(source.params, field_with_brackets) do\\n   236→      #       nil -> nil\\n   237→      #       value -> value\\n   238→      #     end\\n   239→      #   value -> value\\n   240→      # end\\n   241→    end\\n   242→\\n   243→    def encode_form_values(%{impl: Phoenix.HTML.FormData.Ecto.Changeset, source: source}, opts) do\\n   244→      source |> collect_changeset_values(opts) |> LiveVue.Encoder.encode(opts)\\n   245→    end\\n   246→  end\\n   247→\\n   248→  # Fallback for other form implementations (like to_form backed by maps)\\n   249→  def encode_form_values(form, opts) do\\n   250→    base_values =\\n   251→      form.hidden\\n   252→      |> Map.new()\\n   253→      |> Map.merge(form.data)\\n   254→      |> Map.merge(Map.new(form.params))\\n   255→\\n   256→    # For non-changeset forms, we don't have type information for embeds\\n   257→    # so just encode the values as-is\\n   258→    LiveVue.Encoder.encode(base_values, opts)\\n   259→  end\\n   260→\\n   261→  if Code.ensure_loaded?(Ecto) do\\n   262→    defp collect_changeset_errors(%Ecto.Changeset{} = changeset) do\\n   263→      # Collect direct errors from this changeset\\n   264→      errors = translate_errors(changeset.errors)\\n   265→\\n   266→      # Collect errors from nested embedded changesets\\n   267→      Enum.reduce(changeset.changes, errors, fn {field, value}, acc ->\\n   268→        case Map.get(changeset.types, field) do\\n   269→          {tag, %{cardinality: :one}} when tag in @relations ->\\n   270→            # Single embedded changeset\\n   271→            embed_errors = collect_changeset_errors(value)\\n   272→            if embed_errors == %{}, do: acc, else: Map.put(acc, field, embed_errors)\\n   273→\\n   274→          {tag, %{cardinality: :many}} when tag in @relations ->\\n   275→            # List of embedded changesets\\n   276→            # Must filter out changesets with nil params (deleted items) to ensure the errors\\n   277→            # array has the same length and ordering as the values array (see line 210)\\n   278→            list_errors =\\n   279→              value\\n   280→              |> Enum.filter(&(&1.params != nil))\\n   281→              |> Enum.map(fn embed_changeset ->\\n   282→                embed_errors = collect_changeset_errors(embed_changeset)\\n   283→                if embed_errors == %{}, do: nil, else: embed_errors\\n   284→              end)\\n   285→\\n   286→            # Only include the field if there are any errors in the list\\n   287→            if Enum.all?(list_errors, &is_nil/1), do: acc, else: Map.put(acc, field, list_errors)\\n   288→\\n   289→          _ ->\\n   290→            acc\\n   291→        end\\n   292→      end)\\n   293→    end\\n   294→\\n   295→    def encode_form_errors(%{impl: Phoenix.HTML.FormData.Ecto.Changeset} = form) do\\n   296→      collect_changeset_errors(form.source)\\n   297→    end\\n   298→  end\\n   299→\\n   300→  # Fallback for other form implementations (like test mocks)\\n   301→  def encode_form_errors(form) do\\n   302→    translate_errors(form.errors)\\n   303→  end\\n   304→\\n   305→  defp translate_errors(errors) do\\n   306→    Map.new(errors, fn {field, error} -> {field, error |> List.wrap() |> Enum.map(&translate_error/1)} end)\\n   307→  end\\n   308→\\n   309→  defp translate_error({msg, opts}) do\\n   310→    backend = Application.get_env(:live_vue, :gettext_backend, nil)\\n   311→    count = opts[:count]\\n   312→\\n   313→    cond do\\n   314→      backend != nil and count != nil and Code.ensure_loaded?(Gettext) ->\\n   315→        apply(Gettext, :dngettext, [backend, \\\"errors\\\", msg, msg, count, opts])\\n   316→\\n   317→      backend != nil and Code.ensure_loaded?(Gettext) ->\\n   318→        apply(Gettext, :dgettext, [backend, \\\"errors\\\", msg, opts])\\n   319→\\n   320→      true ->\\n   321→        Enum.reduce(opts, msg, fn {key, value}, acc ->\\n   322→          String.replace(acc, \\\"%{#{key}}\\\", value |> List.wrap() |> Enum.map_join(\\\", \\\", &to_string/1))\\n   323→        end)\\n   324→    end\\n   325→  end\\n   326→end\\n   327→\\n   328→# Default implementation for structs - convert to map and encode recursively\\n   329→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Any do\\n   330→  defmacro __deriving__(module, struct, opts) do\\n   331→    fields = fields_to_encode(struct, opts)\\n   332→\\n   333→    quote do\\n   334→      defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: unquote(module) do\\n   335→        def encode(struct, opts) do\\n   336→          struct\\n   337→          |> Map.take(unquote(fields))\\n   338→          |> LiveVue.Encoder.encode(opts)\\n   339→        end\\n   340→      end\\n   341→    end\\n   342→  end\\n   343→\\n   344→  def encode(%{__struct__: module} = struct, _opts) do\\n   345→    raise Protocol.UndefinedError,\\n   346→      protocol: @protocol,\\n   347→      value: struct,\\n   348→      description: \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   349→      LiveVue.Encoder protocol must always be explicitly implemented.\\n   350→\\n   351→      It's used to encode structs to JSON for LiveVue. It's very similar to Jason.Encoder,\\n   352→      but it's converting structs to maps so LiveVue can diff them correctly.\\n   353→\\n   354→      If you own the struct, you can derive the implementation specifying \\\\\\n   355→      which fields should be encoded:\\n   356→\\n   357→          defmodule #{inspect(module)} do\\n   358→            @derive {LiveVue.Encoder, only: [...]}\\n   359→            defstruct ...\\n   360→          end\\n   361→\\n   362→      If you don't own the struct you want to encode, \\\\\\n   363→      you may use Protocol.derive/3 placed outside of any module:\\n   364→\\n   365→          Protocol.derive(LiveVue.Encoder, #{inspect(module)}, only: [...])\\n   366→          Protocol.derive(LiveVue.Encoder, #{inspect(module)})\\n   367→\\n   368→      Nothing prevents you from defining your own implementation for the struct:\\n   369→\\n   370→      defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: #{inspect(module)} do\\n   371→        def encode(struct, opts) do\\n   372→          struct\\n   373→          |> Map.take([:first, :second])\\n   374→          |> LiveVue.Encoder.encode(opts)\\n   375→        end\\n   376→      end\\n   377→      \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n   378→  end\\n   379→\\n   380→  def encode(value, _opts) do\\n   381→    # For any other type, try to pass through as-is\\n   382→    # This covers things like PIDs, references, etc.\\n   383→    value\\n   384→  end\\n   385→\\n   386→  defp fields_to_encode(struct, opts) do\\n   387→    fields = Map.keys(struct)\\n   388→\\n   389→    cond do\\n   390→      only = Keyword.get(opts, :only) ->\\n   391→        case only -- fields do\\n   392→          [] ->\\n   393→            only\\n   394→\\n   395→          error_keys ->\\n   396→            raise ArgumentError,\\n   397→                  \\\":only specified keys (#{inspect(error_keys)}) that are not defined in defstruct: \\\" <>\\n   398→                    \\\"#{inspect(fields -- [:__struct__])}\\\"\\n   399→        end\\n   400→\\n   401→      except = Keyword.get(opts, :except) ->\\n   402→        case except -- fields do\\n   403→          [] ->\\n   404→            fields -- [:__struct__ | except]\\n   405→\\n   406→          error_keys ->\\n   407→            raise ArgumentError,\\n   408→                  \\\":except specified keys (#{inspect(error_keys)}) that are not defined in defstruct: \\\" <>\\n   409→                    \\\"#{inspect(fields -- [:__struct__])}\\\"\\n   410→        end\\n   411→\\n   412→      true ->\\n   413→        fields -- [:__struct__]\\n   414→    end\\n   415→  end\\n   416→end\\n   417→\\n   418→# Phoenix LiveView AsyncResult struct\\n   419→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Phoenix.LiveView.AsyncResult do\\n   420→  def encode(%Phoenix.LiveView.AsyncResult{} = struct, opts) do\\n   421→    LiveVue.Encoder.encode(\\n   422→      %{\\n   423→        ok: struct.ok?,\\n   424→        loading: struct.loading,\\n   425→        failed: encode_failed(struct.failed),\\n   426→        result: struct.result\\n   427→      },\\n   428→      opts\\n   429→    )\\n   430→  end\\n   431→\\n   432→  # Unwrap error tuples for JSON compatibility\\n   433→  defp encode_failed({:error, reason}), do: reason\\n   434→  defp encode_failed({:exit, reason}), do: reason\\n   435→  defp encode_failed(other), do: other\\n   436→end\\n   437→\\n   438→# Phoenix LiveView Upload structs\\n   439→# Explicit implementation of LiveVue.Encoder for UploadConfig\\n   440→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Phoenix.LiveView.UploadConfig do\\n   441→  def encode(%Phoenix.LiveView.UploadConfig{} = struct, opts) do\\n   442→    errors =\\n   443→      Enum.map(struct.errors, fn {key, value} ->\\n   444→        %{ref: key, error: LiveVue.Encoder.encode(value, opts)}\\n   445→      end)\\n   446→\\n   447→    entries =\\n   448→      Enum.map(struct.entries, fn entry ->\\n   449→        encoded = LiveVue.Encoder.encode(entry, opts)\\n   450→        entry_errors = errors |> Enum.filter(&(&1.ref == entry.ref)) |> Enum.map(& &1.error)\\n   451→        Map.put(encoded, :errors, entry_errors)\\n   452→      end)\\n   453→\\n   454→    LiveVue.Encoder.encode(\\n   455→      %{\\n   456→        ref: struct.ref,\\n   457→        name: struct.name,\\n   458→        accept: struct.accept,\\n   459→        max_entries: struct.max_entries,\\n   460→        auto_upload: struct.auto_upload?,\\n   461→        entries: entries,\\n   462→        errors: errors\\n   463→      },\\n   464→      opts\\n   465→    )\\n   466→  end\\n   467→end\\n   468→\\n   469→# Explicit implementation of LiveVue.Encoder for UploadEntry\\n   470→defimpl LiveVue.Encoder, for: Phoenix.LiveView.UploadEntry do\\n   471→  def encode(%Phoenix.LiveView.UploadEntry{} = struct, opts) do\\n   472→    LiveVue.Encoder.encode(\\n   473→      %{\\n   474→        ref: struct.ref,\\n   475→        client_name: struct.client_name,\\n   476→        client_size: struct.client_size,\\n   477→        client_type: struct.client_type,\\n   478→        progress: struct.progress,\\n   479→        done: struct.done?,\\n   480→        valid: struct.valid?,\\n   481→        preflighted: struct.preflighted?\\n   482→      },\\n   483→      opts\\n   484→    )\\n   485→  end\\n   486→end\\n   487→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"7778722b-00df-4ba9-9b2c-1d3a627e7922\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:58.055Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"cc5e722b-015f-452f-a350-f58720bfbaa8\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"7778722b-00df-4ba9-9b2c-1d3a627e7922\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01K4vfESAA425zXcRttv63Cu\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_018o86K4JC9L2mqAYazXUjNj\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue/lib/live_vue/slots.ex\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":3,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":14854,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":14854},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"}},\"requestId\":\"req_011CY1Yn2tJUgmnJYMGC5W8G\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"84ad5c37-6cb4-4321-8e45-ce0873d8df96\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:58.498Z\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"84ad5c37-6cb4-4321-8e45-ce0873d8df96\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"progress\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"hook_progress\",\"hookEvent\":\"PostToolUse\",\"hookName\":\"PostToolUse:Read\",\"command\":\"callback\"},\"parentToolUseID\":\"toolu_018o86K4JC9L2mqAYazXUjNj\",\"toolUseID\":\"toolu_018o86K4JC9L2mqAYazXUjNj\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-02-11T03:10:58.501Z\",\"uuid\":\"77967ab8-3792-4d1d-9685-2072d0f061ad\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"84ad5c37-6cb4-4321-8e45-ce0873d8df96\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"userType\":\"external\",\"cwd\":\"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue\",\"sessionId\":\"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d\",\"version\":\"2.1.32\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"agentId\":\"a1f5895\",\"slug\":\"ticklish-crafting-duckling\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_018o86K4JC9L2mqAYazXUjNj\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"     1→defmodule LiveVue.Slots do\\n     2→  @moduledoc false\\n     3→\\n     4→  import Phoenix.Component\\n     5→\\n     6→  @doc false\\n     7→  def rendered_slot_map(assigns) when assigns == %{}, do: %{}\\n     8→\\n     9→  def rendered_slot_map(assigns) do\\n    10→    for(\\n    11→      {key, [%{__slot__: _}] = slot} <- assigns,\\n    12→      into: %{},\\n    13→      do:\\n    14→        case(key) do\\n    15→          # we raise here because :inner_block is always there and we want to avoid\\n    16→          # it overriding the default slot content\\n    17→          :default -> raise \\\"Instead of using <:default> use <:inner_block> slot\\\"\\n    18→          :inner_block -> {:default, render(%{slot: slot})}\\n    19→          slot_name -> {slot_name, render(%{slot: slot})}\\n    20→        end\\n    21→    )\\n    22→  end\\n    23→\\n    24→  @doc false\\n    25→  def base_encode_64(assigns) do\\n    26→    for {key, value} <- assigns, into: %{}, do: {key, Base.encode64(value)}\\n    27→  end\\n    28→\\n    29→  @doc false\\n    30→  defp render(assigns) do\\n    31→    ~H\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    32→    <%= if assigns[:slot] do %>\\n    33→      {render_slot(@slot)}\\n    34→    <% end %>\\n    35→    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    36→    |> Phoenix.HTML.Safe.to_iodata()\\n    37→    |> List.to_string()\\n    38→    |> String.trim()\\n    39→  end\\n    40→end\\n    41→\\n\\