{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AXT2CNg3cFmsorcqNuiSE7","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# LiveVue End-to-End Tests\n     2→\n     3→This directory contains end-to-end tests for the LiveVue library using Playwright.\n     4→\n     5→## Setup\n     6→\n     7→1. Install Playwright browsers:\n     8→   ```bash\n     9→   npm run e2e:install\n    10→   ```\n    11→\n    12→## Running Tests\n    13→\n    14→```bash\n    15→npm run e2e:test           # Run all tests\n    16→npm run e2e:test:headed    # Run with browser UI\n    17→npm run e2e:test:debug     # Debug interactively\n    18→```\n    19→\n    20→## Structure\n    21→\n    22→Tests are organized as colocated features - each feature has its LiveView, Vue components, and tests in one directory:\n    23→\n    24→```\n    25→test/e2e/\n    26→├── features/\n    27→│   ├── basic/              # Basic counter test\n    28→│   │   ├── live.ex         # LiveView module (LiveVue.E2E.TestLive)\n    29→│   │   ├── counter.vue     # Vue component\n    30→│   │   └── basic.spec.js   # Playwright test\n    31→│   ├── form/               # Form validation tests\n    32→│   ├── stream/             # LiveView streams tests\n    33→│   ├── event/              # useLiveEvent tests\n    34→│   ├── event-reply/        # Event reply tests\n    35→│   ├── navigation/         # useLiveNavigation tests\n    36→│   ├── prop-diff/          # Prop diffing tests\n    37→│   ├── slot/               # Slot rendering tests\n    38→│   └── upload/             # File upload tests\n    39→├── js/\n    40→│   └── app.js              # Vue/LiveSocket bootstrap\n    41→├── test_helper.exs         # Phoenix endpoint, routes, layout\n    42→├── utils.js                # Test utilities\n    43→├── playwright.config.js\n    44→└── vite.config.js\n    45→```\n    46→\n    47→## Adding a New Test Feature\n    48→\n    49→1. Create directory: `test/e2e/features/my-feature/`\n    50→\n    51→2. Add LiveView (`live.ex`):\n    52→   ```elixir\n    53→   defmodule LiveVue.E2E.MyFeatureLive do\n    54→     use Phoenix.LiveView\n    55→\n    56→     def mount(_params, _session, socket) do\n    57→       {:ok, assign(socket, :data, \"hello\")}\n    58→     end\n    59→\n    60→     def render(assigns) do\n    61→       ~H\"\"\"\n    62→       <LiveVue.vue data={@data} v-component=\"my_component\" v-socket={@socket} />\n    63→       \"\"\"\n    64→     end\n    65→   end\n    66→   ```\n    67→\n    68→3. Add Vue component (`my_component.vue`):\n    69→   ```vue\n    70→   <script setup lang=\"ts\">\n    71→   defineProps<{ data: string }>()\n    72→   </script>\n    73→   <template>\n    74→     <div data-testid=\"output\">{{ data }}</div>\n    75→   </template>\n    76→   ```\n    77→\n    78→4. Add route to `test_helper.exs`:\n    79→   ```elixir\n    80→   live \"/my-feature\", MyFeatureLive\n    81→   ```\n    82→\n    83→5. Add test (`my-feature.spec.js`):\n    84→   ```javascript\n    85→   import { test, expect } from \"@playwright/test\"\n    86→   import { syncLV } from \"../../utils.js\"\n    87→\n    88→   test(\"my feature works\", async ({ page }) => {\n    89→     await page.goto(\"/my-feature\")\n    90→     await syncLV(page)\n    91→     await expect(page.getByTestId(\"output\")).toHaveText(\"hello\")\n    92→   })\n    93→   ```\n    94→\n    95→## Test Utilities\n    96→\n    97→- `syncLV(page)` - Wait for LiveView to connect and finish loading\n    98→- `evalLV(page, code)` - Execute Elixir code in LiveView process (returns result)\n    99→\n   100→## Test Server\n   101→\n   102→Runs on http://localhost:4004. Routes are defined in `test_helper.exs`.\n   103→\n   104→## Notes\n   105→\n   106→- LiveView modules are compiled via `elixirc_paths(:e2e)` in `mix.exs`\n   107→- Vue components are discovered via `import.meta.glob(\"../features/**/*.vue\")`\n   108→- Each feature can have multiple Vue components if needed\n   109→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013ZQxJcEnb8yScJd143QixN","session_id":"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d","uuid":"0fa12ef4-bfe8-4111-90ba-cffcfca8959e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014kCUiamfDqkRthC7NYyaAv","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# LiveVue vs Alternatives\n     2→\n     3→This guide compares LiveVue with other Phoenix LiveView frontend integration libraries to help you choose the right solution for your project.\n     4→\n     5→## Overview\n     6→\n     7→LiveVue is part of a growing ecosystem of libraries that integrate modern frontend frameworks with Phoenix LiveView. Each library takes a different approach to solving the challenge of combining server-side state management with rich client-side interactivity.\n     8→\n     9→## Comparison Matrix\n    10→\n    11→| Feature | LiveVue | LiveSvelte | LiveReact | LiveState |\n    12→|---------|---------|------------|-----------|-----------|\n    13→| **Framework** | Vue.js | Svelte | React | Framework-agnostic |\n    14→| **SSR Support** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |\n    15→| **Component Shortcuts** | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |\n    16→| **Slots Support** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |\n    17→| **TypeScript Support** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n    18→| **Build System** | Vite | Custom esbuild | Vite | Any |\n    19→| **Sigil DSL** | ✅ (`~VUE`) | ✅ (`~V`) | ❌ | ❌ |\n    20→| **Embeddable Apps** | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |\n    21→| **Real-time Updates** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n    22→| **Event Handling** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n    23→| **Maturity** | Mature | Mature | Stable | Stable |\n    24→\n    25→## LiveSvelte\n    26→\n    27→[LiveSvelte](https://github.com/woutdp/live_svelte) integrates Svelte with Phoenix LiveView, offering end-to-end reactivity.\n    28→\n    29→### Key Features\n    30→\n    31→- **Sigil DSL**: Use `~V` sigil to write Svelte code directly in LiveView templates\n    32→- **Server-Side Rendering**: Full SSR support with hydration\n    33→- **Slot Interoperability**: Pass LiveView content to Svelte components\n    34→- **Component Macro**: Auto-generate component functions from Svelte files\n    35→- **Preprocessing Support**: Built-in support for TypeScript, SCSS, etc.\n    36→\n    37→### Example Usage\n    38→\n    39→```elixir\n    40→# Component approach\n    41→<.svelte name=\"Counter\" props={%{count: @count}} socket={@socket} />\n    42→\n    43→# Sigil DSL approach\n    44→def render(assigns) do\n    45→  ~V\"\"\"\n    46→  <script>\n    47→    export let count = 0\n    48→    let localState = 1\n    49→    $: combined = count + localState\n    50→  </script>\n    51→\n    52→  <p>Server count: {count}</p>\n    53→  <p>Local state: {localState}</p>\n    54→  <p>Combined: {combined}</p>\n    55→\n    56→  <button phx-click=\"increment\">Server increment</button>\n    57→  <button on:click={() => localState++}>Local increment</button>\n    58→  \"\"\"\n    59→end\n    60→```\n    61→\n    62→### When to Choose LiveSvelte\n    63→\n    64→- You prefer Svelte's syntax and reactivity model\n    65→- You want to use Svelte as an alternative LiveView DSL\n    66→- You need advanced preprocessing (TypeScript, SCSS, etc.)\n    67→- You want the most mature LiveView + frontend framework integration\n    68→- You're building complex animations or transitions\n    69→\n    70→### Considerations\n    71→\n    72→- Custom build system (not standard esbuild)\n    73→- Larger learning curve if unfamiliar with Svelte\n    74→- \"Secret state\" caveat (client code visible to users)\n    75→\n    76→## LiveReact\n    77→\n    78→[LiveReact](https://github.com/mrdotb/live_react) brings React components into Phoenix LiveView applications.\n    79→\n    80→### Key Features\n    81→\n    82→- **SSR Support**: Server-side rendering with hydration\n    83→- **TypeScript Support**: Full TypeScript integration\n    84→- **Vite Integration**: Modern build tooling\n    85→- **Context Provider**: LiveReact context for accessing LiveView functions\n    86→- **Inner Block Slots**: Support for passing content to React components\n    87→\n    88→### Example Usage\n    89→\n    90→```elixir\n    91→<.react name=\"Counter\" props={%{count: @count}} socket={@socket} />\n    92→```\n    93→\n    94→```jsx\n    95→// Counter.jsx\n    96→import { useLiveReact } from 'live_react'\n    97→\n    98→export default function Counter({ count }) {\n    99→  const { pushEvent } = useLiveReact()\n   100→\n   101→  return (\n   102→    <div>\n   103→      <p>Count: {count}</p>\n   104→      <button onClick={() => pushEvent('increment')}>\n   105→        Increment\n   106→      </button>\n   107→    </div>\n   108→  )\n   109→}\n   110→```\n   111→\n   112→### When to Choose LiveReact\n   113→\n   114→- Your team has React expertise\n   115→- You want to reuse existing React components\n   116→- You need access to the vast React ecosystem\n   117→- You prefer JSX syntax\n   118→- You're migrating from a React application\n   119→\n   120→### Considerations\n   121→\n   122→- Newer library (less mature than LiveSvelte)\n   123→- React's larger bundle size\n   124→- More complex state management patterns in React\n   125→\n   126→## LiveState\n   127→\n   128→[LiveState](https://github.com/launchscout/live_state) takes a different approach, focusing on embeddable web applications and framework-agnostic state management.\n   129→\n   130→### Key Features\n   131→\n   132→- **Framework Agnostic**: Works with any frontend framework or vanilla JS\n   133→- **Embeddable Apps**: Designed for building widgets that embed in other sites\n   134→- **Channel-Based**: Uses Phoenix Channels for real-time communication\n   135→- **Event-Driven**: Client dispatches events, server manages state\n   136→- **JSON Patch Updates**: Efficient state synchronization\n   137→\n   138→### Example Usage\n   139→\n   140→```elixir\n   141→# Channel\n   142→defmodule MyApp.TodoChannel do\n   143→  use LiveState.Channel, web_module: MyAppWeb\n   144→\n   145→  def init(_channel, _payload, _socket) do\n   146→    {:ok, %{todos: []}}\n   147→  end\n   148→\n   149→  def handle_event(\"add_todo\", todo, %{todos: todos}) do\n   150→    {:noreply, %{todos: [todo | todos]}}\n   151→  end\n   152→end\n   153→```\n   154→\n   155→```javascript\n   156→// Client (any framework)\n   157→import LiveState from 'phx-live-state'\n   158→\n   159→const liveState = new LiveState({\n   160→  url: 'ws://localhost:4000/socket',\n   161→  topic: 'todos:lobby'\n   162→})\n   163→\n   164→liveState.addEventListener('state_change', (state) => {\n   165→  // Update UI with new state\n   166→})\n   167→\n   168→liveState.dispatchEvent('add_todo', { title: 'New todo' })\n   169→```\n   170→\n   171→### When to Choose LiveState\n   172→\n   173→- You're building embeddable widgets for third-party sites\n   174→- You want framework flexibility (React, Vue, Svelte, vanilla JS)\n   175→- You need real-time updates across multiple clients\n   176→- You're building highly interactive components within existing apps\n   177→- You want minimal client-side complexity\n   178→\n   179→### Considerations\n   180→\n   181→- No SSR support\n   182→- More setup required for basic use cases\n   183→- Different mental model from traditional LiveView\n   184→- Best suited for specific use cases (embeddable apps)\n   185→\n   186→## LiveVue\n   187→\n   188→LiveVue provides seamless Vue.js integration with Phoenix LiveView.\n   189→\n   190→### Key Features\n   191→\n   192→- **End-to-End Reactivity**: Automatic prop updates from LiveView\n   193→- **Server-Side Rendering**: Optional SSR with configurable settings\n   194→- **Component Shortcuts**: Auto-generated component functions\n   195→- **Slot Support**: Pass LiveView content to Vue components\n   196→- **TypeScript Support**: Full TypeScript integration with Vite\n   197→- **Event Handling**: Multiple approaches for handling events\n   198→\n   199→### Example Usage\n   200→\n   201→```elixir\n   202→<.vue\n   203→  v-component=\"Counter\"\n   204→  v-socket={@socket}\n   205→  count={@count}\n   206→  v-on:increment={JS.push(\"inc\")}\n   207→/>\n   208→```\n   209→\n   210→```html\n   211→<!-- Counter.vue -->\n   212→<template>\n   213→  <div>\n   214→    <p>Count: {{ count }}</p>\n   215→    <button @click=\"$emit('increment')\">Increment</button>\n   216→  </div>\n   217→</template>\n   218→\n   219→<script setup>\n   220→defineProps(['count'])\n   221→defineEmits(['increment'])\n   222→</script>\n   223→```\n   224→\n   225→### When to Choose LiveVue\n   226→\n   227→- Your team has Vue.js expertise\n   228→- You want a balance between simplicity and power\n   229→- You prefer Vue's template syntax and reactivity system\n   230→- You need good TypeScript support with modern tooling\n   231→- You want comprehensive documentation and examples\n   232→\n   233→## Decision Framework\n   234→\n   235→### Choose **LiveVue** if:\n   236→- You're experienced with Vue.js\n   237→- You want excellent documentation and examples\n   238→- You need a balance of features and simplicity\n   239→- You prefer Vue's template syntax and composition API\n   240→\n   241→### Choose **LiveSvelte** if:\n   242→- You love Svelte's syntax and reactivity model\n   243→- You want to use frontend framework as LiveView DSL\n   244→- You need the most mature integration\n   245→- You're building animation-heavy applications\n   246→\n   247→### Choose **LiveReact** if:\n   248→- Your team is React-focused\n   249→- You want to reuse existing React components\n   250→- You need access to React's ecosystem\n   251→- You're migrating from a React application\n   252→\n   253→### Choose **LiveState** if:\n   254→- You're building embeddable widgets\n   255→- You need framework flexibility\n   256→- You want real-time updates across clients\n   257→- You're building highly interactive components\n   258→\n   259→### Stick with **Pure LiveView** if:\n   260→- Your app doesn't need complex client-side interactivity\n   261→- You want to minimize JavaScript complexity\n   262→- Your team is primarily backend-focused\n   263→- You're building traditional web applications\n   264→\n   265→## Migration Considerations\n   266→\n   267→### From LiveView to Any Integration\n   268→\n   269→1. **Identify Components**: Determine which parts need client-side interactivity\n   270→2. **State Boundaries**: Decide what state lives on server vs client\n   271→3. **Event Patterns**: Plan how events flow between client and server\n   272→4. **Build Process**: Update your asset pipeline for the chosen framework\n   273→\n   274→### Between Integrations\n   275→\n   276→Most integrations follow similar patterns, making migration feasible:\n   277→\n   278→1. **Component Structure**: All use similar prop-passing patterns\n   279→2. **Event Handling**: All support bidirectional event communication\n   280→3. **SSR**: Most support server-side rendering\n   281→4. **Build Tools**: May require build system changes\n   282→\n   283→## Performance Considerations\n   284→\n   285→### Bundle Size\n   286→- **LiveState**: Smallest (framework-agnostic)\n   287→- **LiveSvelte**: Small (Svelte compiles to minimal code)\n   288→- **LiveVue**: Medium (Vue runtime)\n   289→- **LiveReact**: Largest (React + ReactDOM)\n   290→\n   291→### Runtime Performance\n   292→- **Svelte**: Fastest (compiled, no virtual DOM)\n   293→- **Vue**: Fast (optimized virtual DOM)\n   294→- **React**: Good (virtual DOM with optimizations)\n   295→- **LiveState**: Depends on chosen framework\n   296→\n   297→### Server Load\n   298→All integrations have similar server load characteristics since they use LiveView's WebSocket connection for state synchronization.\n   299→\n   300→## Community and Ecosystem\n   301→\n   302→### LiveSvelte\n   303→- **Maturity**: Most mature integration\n   304→- **Community**: Active development and community\n   305→- **Documentation**: Comprehensive with examples\n   306→\n   307→### LiveVue\n   308→- **Maturity**: Stable and well-documented\n   309→- **Community**: Growing community\n   310→- **Documentation**: Extensive guides and examples\n   311→\n   312→### LiveReact\n   313→- **Maturity**: Recently reached v1.0\n   314→- **Community**: Active development\n   315→- **Documentation**: Good documentation\n   316→\n   317→### LiveState\n   318→- **Maturity**: Stable for specific use cases\n   319→- **Community**: Focused on embeddable apps\n   320→- **Documentation**: Good for target use cases\n   321→\n   322→For current GitHub star counts, check each project's repository directly.\n   323→\n   324→## Conclusion\n   325→\n   326→Each library serves different needs in the Phoenix LiveView ecosystem:\n   327→\n   328→- **LiveVue** offers excellent Vue.js integration with comprehensive features\n   329→- **LiveSvelte** provides the most mature integration with unique DSL capabilities\n   330→- **LiveReact** brings React's ecosystem to LiveView applications\n   331→- **LiveState** enables embeddable applications and framework flexibility\n   332→\n   333→Choose based on your team's expertise, project requirements, and long-term goals. All options provide solid foundations for building interactive web applications with Phoenix LiveView.\n   334→\n   335→## Further Reading\n   336→\n   337→- [LiveVue Documentation](https://hexdocs.pm/live_vue)\n   338→- [LiveSvelte Documentation](https://hexdocs.pm/live_svelte)\n   339→- [LiveReact Documentation](https://hexdocs.pm/live_react)\n   340→- [LiveState Documentation](https://hexdocs.pm/live_state)\n   341→- [Phoenix LiveView Documentation](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix_live_view)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01R45EXfaLjZgcqByia7QETz","session_id":"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d","uuid":"26ea7602-8fda-4983-b864-230b3a87e7ce"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01J6Q7bJaGAVPdMM8fEiBdmY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YMRbFVm9pb6itZirKMggw4","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue/guides/configuration.md"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":223,"cache_read_input_tokens":13137,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":223},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01R45EXfaLjZgcqByia7QETz","session_id":"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d","uuid":"1097d490-c9e9-449a-a9b0-23bb7657ca38"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YMRbFVm9pb6itZirKMggw4","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Configuration\n     2→\n     3→This guide covers all configuration options available in LiveVue, from basic setup to advanced customization.\n     4→\n     5→## Application Configuration\n     6→\n     7→LiveVue configuration is managed in your `config/config.exs` file:\n     8→\n     9→```elixir\n    10→import Config\n    11→\n    12→config :live_vue,\n    13→  # SSR module selection\n    14→  # For development: LiveVue.SSR.ViteJS\n    15→  # For production: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS\n    16→  ssr_module: nil,\n    17→\n    18→  # Default SSR behavior\n    19→  # Can be overridden per-component with v-ssr={true|false}\n    20→  ssr: true,\n    21→\n    22→  # Vite development server URL\n    23→  # Typically http://localhost:5173 in development\n    24→  vite_host: nil,\n    25→\n    26→  # SSR server bundle path (relative to priv directory)\n    27→  # Created by Vite \"build-server\" command\n    28→  ssr_filepath: \"./static/server.mjs\",\n    29→\n    30→  # Testing configuration\n    31→  # When false, we will always update full props and not send diffs\n    32→  # Useful for testing scenarios where you need complete props state\n    33→  enable_props_diff: true,\n    34→\n    35→  # Gettext backend for translating form validation errors\n    36→  # When set, Phoenix.HTML.Form errors are translated using this backend\n    37→  # Example: MyApp.Gettext\n    38→  gettext_backend: nil\n    39→```\n    40→\n    41→### Environment-Specific Configuration\n    42→\n    43→#### Recommended development Configuration\n    44→\n    45→```elixir\n    46→# config/dev.exs\n    47→config :live_vue,\n    48→  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.ViteJS,\n    49→  vite_host: \"http://localhost:5173\",\n    50→  ssr: true\n    51→```\n    52→\n    53→#### Recommended Production Configuration\n    54→\n    55→```elixir\n    56→# config/prod.exs\n    57→config :live_vue,\n    58→  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS,\n    59→  ssr: true\n    60→\n    61→# or if you don't want to use SSR\n    62→config :live_vue,\n    63→  ssr_module: nil,\n    64→  ssr: false\n    65→```\n    66→\n    67→## Vue Application Setup\n    68→\n    69→Configure your Vue application in `assets/vue/index.js`. You should use createLiveVue to provide two required functions:\n    70→\n    71→| Option | Type | Description |\n    72→|--------|------|-------------|\n    73→| `resolve` | `(name: string) => Component \\| Promise<Component>` | Component resolution function |\n    74→| `setup` | `(context: SetupContext) => VueApp` | Vue app setup function |\n    75→\n    76→Installation step provides a reasonable implementation of createLiveVue that you can use as a starting point.\n    77→\n    78→### Basic Configuration\n    79→\n    80→```javascript\n    81→import \"vite/modulepreload-polyfill\"\n    82→import { h } from \"vue\"\n    83→import { createLiveVue, findComponent } from \"live_vue\"\n    84→\n    85→export default createLiveVue({\n    86→  // Component resolution - adjust this to your needs\n    87→  // Eg. You might want to import some components directly from node_modules\n    88→  // or lazy load components\n    89→  resolve: name => {\n    90→    const components = {\n    91→      ...import.meta.glob(\"./**/*.vue\", { eager: true }),\n    92→      ...import.meta.glob(\"../../lib/**/*.vue\", { eager: true }),\n    93→    }\n    94→\n    95→    // findComponent resolves by matching path segments from the end.\n    96→    // It handles both Component.vue and Component/index.vue patterns,\n    97→    // and throws helpful errors for not-found or ambiguous matches.\n    98→    return findComponent(components, name)\n    99→  },\n   100→\n   101→  // Vue app setup\n   102→  setup: ({ createApp, component, props, slots, plugin, el }) => {\n   103→    const app = createApp({ render: () => h(component, props, slots) })\n   104→    app.use(plugin)\n   105→    app.mount(el)\n   106→    return app\n   107→  },\n   108→})\n   109→```\n   110→\n   111→### SetupContext\n   112→\n   113→SetupContext is an object that is passed to the setup function.\n   114→\n   115→| Property | Type | Description |\n   116→|----------|------|-------------|\n   117→| `createApp` | `Function` | Vue's createApp or createSSRApp |\n   118→| `component` | `Component` | The Vue component to render |\n   119→| `props` | `object` | Props passed from LiveView |\n   120→| `slots` | `object` | Slots passed from LiveView |\n   121→| `plugin` | `Plugin` | LiveVue plugin (required) |\n   122→| `el` | `HTMLElement` | Mount target element |\n   123→| `ssr` | `boolean` | Whether this is SSR context |\n   124→\n   125→\n   126→### Component Resolution Options\n   127→\n   128→#### Eager Loading (Default)\n   129→All components are bundled with the main application:\n   130→\n   131→```javascript\n   132→const components = {\n   133→  ...import.meta.glob(\"./**/*.vue\", { eager: true }),\n   134→}\n   135→```\n   136→\n   137→#### Lazy Loading\n   138→If you want to lazy load components, you can use the `import` function:\n   139→\n   140→```javascript\n   141→const components = {\n   142→  Counter: () => import(\"./Counter.vue\"),\n   143→  Modal: () => import(\"./Modal.vue\")\n   144→}\n   145→\n   146→// Or using Vite's glob import\n   147→// useful if we colocate Vue components with LiveView components and want to put each of them into a separate chunk\n   148→// all shared components imported by top-level components will be included as well.\n   149→const components = import.meta.glob(\n   150→  \"../../lib/**/*.vue\",\n   151→  { eager: false, import: 'default' }\n   152→)\n   153→```\n   154→\n   155→#### Custom Resolution Logic\n   156→\n   157→```javascript\n   158→resolve: name => {\n   159→  // Custom component mapping and lazy loading\n   160→  const componentMap = {\n   161→    'MyCounter': () => import('./components/Counter.vue'),\n   162→    'admin/Dashboard': () => import('./admin/Dashboard.vue')\n   163→  }\n   164→\n   165→  return componentMap[name]\n   166→}\n   167→```\n   168→\n   169→### Vue App Customization\n   170→\n   171→Add plugins, stores, and other Vue features:\n   172→\n   173→```javascript\n   174→import { createPinia } from \"pinia\"\n   175→import { createI18n } from \"vue-i18n\"\n   176→\n   177→export default createLiveVue({\n   178→  setup: ({ createApp, component, props, slots, plugin, el, ssr }) => {\n   179→    const app = createApp({ render: () => h(component, props, slots) })\n   180→\n   181→    // LiveVue plugin (required)\n   182→    app.use(plugin)\n   183→\n   184→    // Add your plugins\n   185→    const pinia = createPinia()\n   186→    app.use(pinia)\n   187→\n   188→    const i18n = createI18n({\n   189→      locale: 'en',\n   190→      messages: { /* your translations */ }\n   191→    })\n   192→    app.use(i18n)\n   193→\n   194→    // SSR-specific setup\n   195→    if (ssr) {\n   196→      // Server-side specific initialization\n   197→    }\n   198→\n   199→    app.mount(el)\n   200→    return app\n   201→  }\n   202→})\n   203→```\n   204→\n   205→## Component Organization\n   206→\n   207→### Directory Structure\n   208→\n   209→By default, Vue components are resolved from:\n   210→- `assets/vue/` - Main Vue components directory\n   211→- `lib/my_app_web/` - Colocated with LiveView files\n   212→\n   213→### Custom Vue Root Directories\n   214→\n   215→Configure component discovery paths in your LiveView module:\n   216→\n   217→```elixir\n   218→# lib/my_app_web.ex\n   219→defmodule MyAppWeb do\n   220→  def html_helpers do\n   221→    quote do\n   222→      use LiveVue.Components, vue_root: [\n   223→        \"./assets/vue\",\n   224→        \"./lib/my_app_web\",\n   225→        \"./lib/my_app_web/components\"\n   226→      ]\n   227→    end\n   228→  end\n   229→end\n   230→```\n   231→\n   232→This generates shortcut functions for your components:\n   233→\n   234→```elixir\n   235→# Instead of\n   236→<.vue v-component=\"Counter\" v-socket={@socket} />\n   237→\n   238→# You can use\n   239→<.Counter v-socket={@socket} />\n   240→```\n   241→\n   242→### Component Naming Conventions\n   243→\n   244→Components are resolved by name or path suffix:\n   245→- `Counter.vue` → accessible as `\"Counter\"`\n   246→- `path/to/Component.vue` → accessible as `\"path/to/Component\"` or `\"Component\"`\n   247→- `path/to/component/index.vue` → accessible as `\"path/to/component\"` or `\"component\"`\n   248→\n   249→## Server-Side Rendering (SSR)\n   250→\n   251→LiveVue provides flexible SSR options that work great in both development and production environments.\n   252→\n   253→### SSR Modules\n   254→\n   255→LiveVue offers two SSR strategies depending on your environment:\n   256→\n   257→#### ViteJS (Development)\n   258→Perfect for development with hot module replacement:\n   259→\n   260→```elixir\n   261→# config/dev.exs\n   262→config :live_vue,\n   263→  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.ViteJS,\n   264→  vite_host: \"http://localhost:5173\"\n   265→```\n   266→\n   267→Uses Vite's `ssrLoadModule` for efficient development compilation with instant updates.\n   268→\n   269→#### NodeJS (Production)\n   270→Optimized for production with an in-memory server bundle:\n   271→\n   272→```elixir\n   273→# config/prod.exs\n   274→config :live_vue,\n   275→  ssr_module: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS,\n   276→  ssr: true\n   277→```\n   278→\n   279→Uses elixir-nodejs with a pre-built server bundle for optimal performance.\n   280→\n   281→### SSR Configuration\n   282→\n   283→Control SSR behavior globally or per-component:\n   284→\n   285→```elixir\n   286→# Global SSR settings\n   287→config :live_vue,\n   288→  ssr: true,  # Enable SSR by default\n   289→  ssr_filepath: \"./static/server.mjs\"  # Server bundle path\n   290→```\n   291→\n   292→### SSR Behavior\n   293→\n   294→SSR is intelligently applied:\n   295→- **Runs during**: Initial page loads (dead renders)\n   296→- **Skips during**: Live navigation and WebSocket updates\n   297→- **Can be disabled**: Per-component with `v-ssr={false}`\n   298→\n   299→> #### What are \"dead renders\"? {: .info}\n   300→>\n   301→> A \"dead render\" occurs when the page is loaded without an active WebSocket connection - this includes the initial HTTP request before LiveView connects. During this phase, SSR renders the Vue component to HTML on the server so users see content immediately.\n   302→>\n   303→> Once the WebSocket connects (making the view \"live\"), SSR is skipped because Vue components are already mounted and hydrated client-side. This means subsequent prop updates go directly to the mounted Vue instance without re-running SSR.\n   304→\n   305→This gives you the SEO and performance benefits of SSR without the overhead during live updates.\n   306→\n   307→### Per-Component SSR Control\n   308→\n   309→Override global settings for specific components:\n   310→\n   311→```elixir\n   312→<!-- Force SSR for this component -->\n   313→<.vue v-component=\"CriticalContent\" v-ssr={true} v-socket={@socket} />\n   314→\n   315→<!-- Disable SSR for client-only widgets -->\n   316→<.vue v-component=\"InteractiveChart\" v-ssr={false} v-socket={@socket} />\n   317→\n   318→<!-- Use global default -->\n   319→<.vue v-component=\"RegularComponent\" v-socket={@socket} />\n   320→```\n   321→\n   322→## Testing Configuration\n   323→\n   324→LiveVue provides testing-specific configuration options to help with component testing and debugging.\n   325→\n   326→### enable_props_diff\n   327→\n   328→By default, LiveVue optimizes performance by only sending prop changes (diffs) to the client. However, during testing, you may need access to the complete props state rather than just the incremental changes.\n   329→\n   330→```elixir\n   331→# config/test.exs\n   332→config :live_vue,\n   333→  enable_props_diff: false\n   334→```\n   335→\n   336→When disabled:\n   337→- LiveVue will always send full props and not send diffs\n   338→- The `props` field returned by `LiveVue.Test.get_vue/2` will contain the complete props state\n   339→- This makes it easier to write comprehensive tests that verify the full component state\n   340→- Useful for debugging component behavior and ensuring all props are correctly passed\n   341→\n   342→**Note**: This option is primarily intended for testing scenarios. In production, the default behavior (sending only diffs) provides better performance.\n   343→\n   344→### SSR Performance\n   345→\n   346→Vue SSR is compiled into optimized string concatenation for maximum performance. The SSR process:\n   347→- Only runs during \"dead\" renders (no WebSocket connection)\n   348→- Skips during live navigation for better UX\n   349→- Can be disabled per-component when not needed\n   350→\n   351→### Production SSR Setup\n   352→\n   353→For production deployments, you'll need Node.js 19+ and proper configuration:\n   354→\n   355→1. **Install Node.js 19+** in your production environment\n   356→2. **Configure NodeJS supervisor** in your `application.ex`:\n   357→\n   358→```elixir\n   359→children = [\n   360→  {NodeJS.Supervisor, [path: LiveVue.SSR.NodeJS.server_path(), pool_size: 4]},\n   361→  # ... other children\n   362→]\n   363→```\n   364→\n   365→3. **Build server bundle** as part of your deployment:\n   366→\n   367→```bash\n   368→# In your deployment script\n   369→cd assets && npm run build-server\n   370→```\n   371→\n   372→The server bundle will be created at `priv/static/server.mjs` and used by the NodeJS supervisor.\n   373→\n   374→### SSR Troubleshooting\n   375→\n   376→**SSR not working in development?**\n   377→- Check that Vite dev server is running on the configured port\n   378→- Verify `vite_host` matches your Vite server URL\n   379→\n   380→**SSR failing in production?**\n   381→- Ensure Node.js 19+ is installed\n   382→- Check that `priv/static/server.mjs` exists after build\n   383→- Verify NodeJS supervisor is properly configured\n   384→\n   385→**Performance issues?**\n   386→- Consider adjusting the NodeJS pool size based on your server capacity\n   387→- Disable SSR for components that don't benefit from it\n   388→\n   389→## Shared Props (Removed)\n   390→\n   391→> **Note**: The `shared_props` configuration option was removed in v1.0.0.\n   392→>\n   393→> This feature allowed automatic injection of socket assigns into all Vue components.\n   394→> However, it had a fundamental flaw: LiveView only re-renders components when their\n   395→> explicitly-passed assigns change. Since shared props were injected at render time\n   396→> (not passed explicitly in the template), changes to shared props would not trigger\n   397→> component re-renders.\n   398→>\n   399→> **Workaround**: Pass props explicitly in your templates:\n   400→> ```elixir\n   401→> <.vue v-component=\"MyComponent\" v-socket={@socket} flash={@flash} user={@current_user} />\n   402→> ```\n   403→>\n   404→> This ensures LiveView's change tracking works correctly and your Vue components\n   405→> receive updates when the data changes.\n   406→\n   407→\n   408→## Troubleshooting Configuration\n   409→\n   410→### Common Issues\n   411→\n   412→1. **Components not found**: Check `vue_root` paths in `LiveVue.Components`\n   413→2. **SSR errors**: Verify `ssr_module` and `vite_host` configuration\n   414→3. **TypeScript errors**: Ensure proper `tsconfig.json` setup\n   415→4. **Build failures**: Check Vite configuration and entry points\n   416→\n   417→### Debug Configuration\n   418→\n   419→Enable debug logging:\n   420→\n   421→```elixir\n   422→config :logger, level: :debug\n   423→\n   424→# In your component\n   425→require Logger\n   426→Logger.debug(\"Vue component props: #{inspect(props)}\")\n   427→\n   428→# on the frontend, use VueDevTools to debug\n   429→```\n   430→\n   431→## Next Steps\n   432→\n   433→With your configuration complete, explore:\n   434→- [Getting Started](getting_started.md) for your first component\n   435→- [Basic Usage](basic_usage.md) for common patterns\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01R45EXfaLjZgcqByia7QETz","session_id":"64a1054d-d5d1-470b-988c-6d3926af5d5d","uuid":"e74055a6-28ff-465f-8799-bc808376c631"}
