{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NvzBu7bhtpxHZKz3tzE3sm","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Basic Usage\n     2→\n     3→This guide covers the fundamental patterns for using Vue components within LiveView.\n     4→\n     5→## Component Organization\n     6→\n     7→By default, Vue components should be placed in either:\n     8→- `assets/vue` directory\n     9→- Colocated with your LiveView files in `lib/my_app_web`\n    10→\n    11→For advanced component organization and custom resolution patterns, see [Configuration](configuration.md#component-organization).\n    12→\n    13→## Rendering Components\n    14→\n    15→### Basic Syntax\n    16→\n    17→To render a Vue component from HEEX, use the `<.vue>` function:\n    18→\n    19→```elixir\n    20→<.vue\n    21→  count={@count}\n    22→  v-component=\"Counter\"\n    23→  v-socket={@socket}\n    24→  v-on:inc={JS.push(\"inc\")}\n    25→/>\n    26→```\n    27→\n    28→### Required Attributes\n    29→\n    30→| Attribute    | Example                | Required        | Description                                    |\n    31→|--------------|------------------------|-----------------|------------------------------------------------|\n    32→| v-component  | `v-component=\"Counter\"`| Yes            | Component name or path relative to vue_root    |\n    33→| v-socket     | `v-socket={@socket}`   | Yes in LiveView| Required for SSR and reactivity               |\n    34→\n    35→### Optional Attributes\n    36→\n    37→| Attribute    | Example              | Description                                    |\n    38→|--------------|----------------------|------------------------------------------------|\n    39→| v-ssr        | `v-ssr={true}`      | Override default SSR setting                   |\n    40→| v-on:event   | `v-on:inc={JS.push(\"inc\")}` | Handle Vue component events           |\n    41→| prop={@value}| `count={@count}`     | Pass props to the component                   |\n    42→\n    43→### Component Shortcut\n    44→\n    45→Instead of writing `<.vue v-component=\"Counter\">`, you can use the shortcut syntax:\n    46→\n    47→```elixir\n    48→<.Counter count={@count} v-socket={@socket} />\n    49→```\n    50→\n    51→Function names are generated based on `.vue` file names. For files with identical names, use the full path:\n    52→\n    53→```elixir\n    54→<.vue v-component=\"helpers/nested/Modal\" />\n    55→```\n    56→\n    57→## Passing Props\n    58→\n    59→Props can be passed in three equivalent ways:\n    60→\n    61→```elixir\n    62→# Individual props\n    63→<.vue count={@count} max={123} v-component=\"Counter\" v-socket={@socket} />\n    64→\n    65→# Map spread\n    66→<.vue v-component=\"Counter\" v-socket={@socket} {@props} />\n    67→\n    68→# Using shortcut - you don't have to specify v-component\n    69→<.Counter count={@count} max={123} v-socket={@socket} />\n    70→```\n    71→\n    72→### Custom Structs as Props\n    73→\n    74→When passing custom structs as props, you must implement the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol:\n    75→\n    76→```elixir\n    77→defmodule User do\n    78→  @derive LiveVue.Encoder\n    79→  defstruct [:name, :email, :age]\n    80→end\n    81→\n    82→# Use in your LiveView\n    83→def render(assigns) do\n    84→  ~H\"\"\"\n    85→  <.vue\n    86→    user={@current_user}\n    87→    v-component=\"UserProfile\"\n    88→    v-socket={@socket}\n    89→  />\n    90→  \"\"\"\n    91→end\n    92→```\n    93→\n    94→The encoder protocol ensures that only specified fields are sent to the client, sensitive data is protected, and props can be efficiently diffed for updates.\n    95→\n    96→For complete implementation details including field selection and custom implementations, see [Component Reference](component_reference.md#custom-structs-with-livevue-encoder).\n    97→\n    98→> #### Protocol.UndefinedError {: .warning}\n    99→>\n   100→> If you get a `Protocol.UndefinedError` when passing structs as props, it means you need to implement the `LiveVue.Encoder` protocol for that struct. This is a safety feature to prevent accidental exposure of sensitive data.\n   101→\n   102→## Handling Events\n   103→\n   104→### Phoenix Events\n   105→\n   106→All standard Phoenix event handlers work inside Vue components:\n   107→- `phx-click`\n   108→- `phx-change`\n   109→- `phx-submit`\n   110→- etc.\n   111→\n   112→They will be pushed directly to LiveView, exactly as happens with `HEEX` components.\n   113→\n   114→### Programmatic access to hook instance\n   115→\n   116→There are two ways to access the Phoenix LiveView hook instance from your Vue components:\n   117→\n   118→1.  **`useLiveVue()` Composable (in `<script setup>`):**\n   119→\n   120→    Use the `useLiveVue()` composable when you need to access the hook instance for logic within your `<script setup>` block. It's ideal for pushing events programmatically.\n   121→\n   122→    ```html\n   123→    <script setup>\n   124→    import { useLiveVue } from \"live_vue\"\n   125→    import { ref } from \"vue\"\n   126→\n   127→    const live = useLiveVue()\n   128→    const name = ref(\"\")\n   129→\n   130→    function save() {\n   131→      live.pushEvent(\"save\", { name: name.value })\n   132→    }\n   133→    </script>\n   134→    ```\n   135→\n   136→    To listen for events from the server, the easiest way is to use the `useLiveEvent` composable, which will automatically handle cleanup for you.\n   137→\n   138→    ```html\n   139→    <script setup>\n   140→    import { useLiveEvent } from \"live_vue\"\n   141→\n   142→    // Example: listening for a server event\n   143→    useLiveEvent(\"response\", (payload) => { console.log(payload) })\n   144→    </script>\n   145→    ```\n   146→\n   147→2.  **`$live` Property (in `<template>`):**\n   148→\n   149→    For convenience, the hook instance is also available directly in your template as the `$live` property. This is the preferred method for simple, one-off event pushes directly from the template, as it avoids the need to import and call `useLiveVue()`.\n   150→\n   151→    ```html\n   152→    <template>\n   153→      <button @click=\"$live.pushEvent('hello', { value: 'world' })\">\n   154→        Click me\n   155→      </button>\n   156→    </template>\n   157→    ```\n   158→\n   159→The `live` object provides all methods from [Phoenix.LiveView JS Interop](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix_live_view/js-interop.html#client-hooks-via-phx-hook). For a complete API reference, see [Client-Side API](client_api.md).\n   160→\n   161→### LiveView Navigation\n   162→\n   163→For navigation, LiveVue provides a built-in `Link` component that makes using `live_patch` and `live_redirect` as easy as using a standard `<a>` tag.\n   164→\n   165→```html\n   166→<script setup>\n   167→import { Link } from \"live_vue\"\n   168→</script>\n   169→\n   170→<template>\n   171→  <nav>\n   172→    <!-- Behaves like a normal link -->\n   173→    <Link href=\"/about\">About</Link>\n   174→\n   175→    <!-- Performs a `live_patch` -->\n   176→    <Link patch=\"/users?sort=name\">Sort by Name</Link>\n   177→\n   178→    <!-- Performs a `live_redirect` -->\n   179→    <Link navigate=\"/dashboard\">Dashboard</Link>\n   180→  </nav>\n   181→</template>\n   182→```\n   183→\n   184→For a complete API reference, see [Client-Side API](client_api.md#link).\n   185→\n   186→### Vue Events\n   187→\n   188→If you want to create reusable Vue components where you'd like to define what happens when Vue emits an event, you can use the `v-on:` syntax with `JS` [module helpers](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix_live_view/Phoenix.LiveView.JS.html#module-client-utility-commands).\n   189→\n   190→```elixir\n   191→<.vue\n   192→  v-on:submit={JS.push(\"submit\")}\n   193→  v-on:close={JS.hide()}\n   194→  v-component=\"Form\"\n   195→  v-socket={@socket}\n   196→/>\n   197→```\n   198→\n   199→Special case: When using `JS.push()` without a value, it automatically uses the emit payload:\n   200→```elixir\n   201→# In Vue\n   202→emit('inc', {value: 5})\n   203→\n   204→# In LiveView\n   205→<.vue v-on:inc={JS.push(\"inc\")} />\n   206→# Equivalent to: JS.push(\"inc\", value: 5)\n   207→```\n   208→\n   209→## Slots Support\n   210→\n   211→Vue components can receive slots from LiveView templates:\n   212→\n   213→```elixir\n   214→<.Card title=\"Example Card\" v-socket={@socket}>\n   215→  <p>This is the default slot content!</p>\n   216→  <p>Phoenix components work too: <.icon name=\"hero-info\" /></p>\n   217→\n   218→  <:footer>\n   219→    This is a named slot\n   220→  </:footer>\n   221→</.Card>\n   222→```\n   223→\n   224→```html\n   225→<template>\n   226→  <div>\n   227→    <!-- Default slot -->\n   228→    <slot></slot>\n   229→\n   230→    <!-- Named slot -->\n   231→    <slot name=\"footer\"></slot>\n   232→  </div>\n   233→</template>\n   234→```\n   235→\n   236→Important notes about slots:\n   237→- Each slot is wrapped in a div (technical limitation)\n   238→- You can use HEEX components inside slots 🥳\n   239→- Slots stay reactive and update when their content changes\n   240→\n   241→\n   242→> #### Hooks inside slots are not supported {: .warning}\n   243→>\n   244→> Slots are rendered server-side and then sent to the client as a raw HTML.\n   245→> It happens outside of the LiveView lifecycle, so hooks inside slots are not supported.\n   246→>\n   247→> As a consequence, since `.vue` components rely on hooks, it's not possible to nest `.vue` components inside other `.vue` components.\n   248→\n   249→## File Uploads\n   250→\n   251→LiveVue provides seamless integration with Phoenix LiveView's file upload system through the `useLiveUpload()` composable. This handles all the complexity of managing upload state, progress tracking, and DOM elements automatically.\n   252→\n   253→### Server Setup\n   254→\n   255→First, configure your LiveView with `allow_upload`:\n   256→\n   257→```elixir\n   258→defmodule MyAppWeb.UploadLive do\n   259→  use MyAppWeb, :live_view\n   260→\n   261→  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do\n   262→    {:ok,\n   263→     socket\n   264→     |> assign(:uploaded_files, [])\n   265→     |> allow_upload(:documents,\n   266→       accept: ~w(.pdf .txt .jpg .png),\n   267→       max_entries: 3,\n   268→       max_file_size: 5_000_000,  # 5MB\n   269→       auto_upload: true          # Files upload immediately when selected\n   270→     )}\n   271→  end\n   272→\n   273→  def handle_event(\"validate\", _params, socket) do\n   274→    {:noreply, socket}\n   275→  end\n   276→\n   277→  def handle_event(\"save\", _params, socket) do\n   278→    uploaded_files =\n   279→      consume_uploaded_entries(socket, :documents, fn %{path: path}, entry ->\n   280→        # Process your file here\n   281→        dest = Path.join(\"uploads\", entry.client_name)\n   282→        File.cp!(path, dest)\n   283→        {:ok, %{name: entry.client_name, size: entry.client_size}}\n   284→      end)\n   285→\n   286→    {:noreply, update(socket, :uploaded_files, &(&1 ++ uploaded_files))}\n   287→  end\n   288→\n   289→  def render(assigns) do\n   290→    ~H\"\"\"\n   291→    <div>\n   292→      <.vue\n   293→        upload={@uploads.documents}\n   294→        uploaded_files={@uploaded_files}\n   295→        v-component=\"FileUploader\"\n   296→        v-socket={@socket}\n   297→      />\n   298→    </div>\n   299→    \"\"\"\n   300→  end\n   301→end\n   302→```\n   303→\n   304→### Vue Component\n   305→\n   306→Create a Vue component that uses `useLiveUpload()`:\n   307→\n   308→```html\n   309→<!-- assets/vue/FileUploader.vue -->\n   310→<script setup lang=\"ts\">\n   311→import { useLiveUpload, UploadConfig } from 'live_vue'\n   312→\n   313→interface Props {\n   314→  upload: UploadConfig\n   315→  uploadedFiles: { name: string; size: number }[]\n   316→}\n   317→\n   318→const props = defineProps<Props>()\n   319→\n   320→const {\n   321→  entries,\n   322→  showFilePicker,\n   323→  submit,\n   324→  cancel,\n   325→  progress,\n   326→  valid\n   327→} = useLiveUpload(() => props.upload, {\n   328→  changeEvent: \"validate\",  // Optional: for file validation\n   329→  submitEvent: \"save\"       // Required: for processing uploads\n   330→})\n   331→</script>\n   332→\n   333→<template>\n   334→  <div class=\"upload-container\">\n   335→    <!-- Upload controls -->\n   336→    <div class=\"controls\">\n   337→      <button @click=\"showFilePicker\" class=\"btn-primary\">\n   338→        Choose Files\n   339→      </button>\n   340→\n   341→      <!-- Manual upload button - only shown when auto_upload is false -->\n   342→      <!-- In this example, auto_upload: true is set on the server, so this won't display -->\n   343→      <!-- Include this pattern when you want to support both auto and manual upload modes -->\n   344→      <button\n   345→        v-if=\"!props.upload.auto_upload && entries.length > 0\"\n   346→        @click=\"submit\"\n   347→        class=\"btn-success\"\n   348→      >\n   349→        Upload Files\n   350→      </button>\n   351→\n   352→      <!-- Cancel all -->\n   353→      <button\n   354→        v-if=\"entries.length > 0\"\n   355→        @click=\"cancel()\"\n   356→        class=\"btn-danger\"\n   357→      >\n   358→        Cancel All\n   359→      </button>\n   360→    </div>\n   361→\n   362→    <!-- Progress indicator -->\n   363→    <div v-if=\"entries.length > 0\" class=\"progress\">\n   364→      Overall Progress: {{ progress }}%\n   365→    </div>\n   366→\n   367→    <!-- File list -->\n   368→    <div class=\"file-list\">\n   369→      <div v-for=\"entry in entries\" :key=\"entry.ref\" class=\"file-entry\">\n   370→        <div class=\"file-info\">\n   371→          <span class=\"name\">{{ entry.client_name }}</span>\n   372→          <span class=\"size\">{{ entry.client_size }} bytes</span>\n   373→          <span class=\"progress\">{{ entry.progress }}%</span>\n   374→          <span :class=\"entry.done ? 'done' : 'pending'\">\n   375→            {{ entry.done ? 'Complete' : 'Uploading...' }}\n   376→          </span>\n   377→        </div>\n   378→\n   379→        <!-- Individual file errors -->\n   380→        <div v-if=\"entry.errors?.length\" class=\"errors\">\n   381→          <div v-for=\"error in entry.errors\" :key=\"error\">{{ error }}</div>\n   382→        </div>\n   383→\n   384→        <button @click=\"cancel(entry.ref)\" class=\"cancel-btn\">×</button>\n   385→      </div>\n   386→    </div>\n   387→\n   388→    <!-- Uploaded files -->\n   389→    <div v-if=\"uploadedFiles.length\" class=\"uploaded-files\">\n   390→      <h3>Uploaded Files</h3>\n   391→      <div v-for=\"file in uploadedFiles\" :key=\"file.name\">\n   392→        {{ file.name }} ({{ file.size }} bytes)\n   393→      </div>\n   394→    </div>\n   395→  </div>\n   396→</template>\n   397→```\n   398→\n   399→### Key Features\n   400→\n   401→- **Automatic DOM management**: The composable creates and manages the required file input elements\n   402→- **Progress tracking**: Real-time progress updates for individual files and overall progress\n   403→- **Error handling**: Validation errors are automatically displayed\n   404→- **Auto-upload support**: Files can upload immediately when selected, or manually triggered\n   405→- **Drag & drop**: Use `addFiles()` method to support drag-and-drop functionality\n   406→- **Cancellation**: Cancel individual files or all uploads\n   407→\n   408→For the complete API reference, see [`useLiveUpload()` in the Client API guide](client_api.md#useliveuploaduploadconfig-options).\n   409→\n   410→## Phoenix Streams\n   411→\n   412→LiveVue provides full support for Phoenix LiveView's `stream()` operations. Since LiveVue already sends minimal JSON patches for all props updates, streams primarily help reduce server memory consumption by not keeping large collections in the socket assigns.\n   413→\n   414→### Why Use Streams?\n   415→\n   416→Streams are ideal for:\n   417→- Lists with many items where you want to avoid keeping all data in memory\n   418→- Real-time data like chat messages, notifications, or live feeds\n   419→\n   420→Note: LiveVue's automatic JSON patch diffing already ensures efficient client updates for regular props, so the main benefit of streams is server-side memory efficiency.\n   421→\n   422→### Server Setup\n   423→\n   424→Configure your LiveView to use streams:\n   425→\n   426→```elixir\n   427→defmodule MyAppWeb.ItemsLive do\n   428→  use MyAppWeb, :live_view\n   429→\n   430→  def render(assigns) do\n   431→    ~H\"\"\"\n   432→    <.vue items={@streams.items} v-component=\"ItemList\" v-socket={@socket} />\n   433→    \"\"\"\n   434→  end\n   435→\n   436→  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do\n   437→    # Initialize with sample items\n   438→    items = [\n   439→      %{id: 1, name: \"Item 1\"},\n   440→      %{id: 2, name: \"Item 2\"},\n   441→      %{id: 3, name: \"Item 3\"}\n   442→    ]\n   443→\n   444→    {:ok, stream(socket, :items, items)}\n   445→  end\n   446→\n   447→  def handle_event(\"add_item\", %{\"name\" => name}, socket) do\n   448→    new_item = %{ id: Enum.random(1..1000), name: name }\n   449→    {:noreply, stream_insert(socket, :items, new_item)}\n   450→  end\n   451→\n   452→  def handle_event(\"remove_item\", %{\"id\" => id}, socket) do\n   453→    {:noreply, stream_delete_by_dom_id(socket, :items, \"item-#{id}\")}\n   454→  end\n   455→\n   456→end\n   457→```\n   458→\n   459→### Vue Component\n   460→\n   461→Create a Vue component that receives and renders the stream:\n   462→\n   463→```html\n   464→<!-- assets/vue/ItemList.vue -->\n   465→<script setup lang=\"ts\">\n   466→import { useLiveVue } from 'live_vue'\n   467→import { ref } from 'vue'\n   468→\n   469→const props = defineProps<{\n   470→  items: {\n   471→    id: number\n   472→    name: string\n   473→  }[]\n   474→}>()\n   475→\n   476→const live = useLiveVue()\n   477→const newName = ref('')\n   478→\n   479→function addItem() {\n   480→  if (newName.value) {\n   481→    // Use $live to push events to LiveView\n   482→    live.pushEvent('add_item', { name: newName.value })\n   483→    newName.value = ''\n   484→  }\n   485→}\n   486→</script>\n   487→\n   488→<template>\n   489→  <div>\n   490→    <!-- Add new item form -->\n   491→    <div>\n   492→      <input v-model=\"newName\" placeholder=\"Item name\" />\n   493→      <button @click=\"addItem\">Add Item</button>\n   494→    </div>\n   495→\n   496→    <!-- Items list -->\n   497→    <div>\n   498→      <div v-for=\"item in items\" :key=\"item.id\" >\n   499→        <h3>{{ item.name }}</h3>\n   500→        <button @click=\"$live.pushEvent('remove_item', { id: item.id })\">\n   501→          Remove\n   502→        </button>\n   503→      </div>\n   504→    </div>\n   505→    <div v-if=\"items.length === 0\" >No items yet.</div>\n   506→  </div>\n   507→</template>\n   508→```\n   509→\n   510→### Key Features\n   511→\n   512→- **Memory Efficient**: Reduces server memory usage by not storing large collections in socket assigns\n   513→- **Transparent Updates**: When you use `stream_insert()`, `stream_delete()`, or other stream operations, LiveVue automatically patches only the affected items\n   514→- **State Preservation**: Vue component state (like form inputs, local variables) is preserved during stream updates\n   515→- **Same API as HEEX**: Use `@streams.items` exactly as you would in a HEEX template\n   516→- **Automatic Patches**: LiveVue's existing JSON patch system handles efficient client updates\n   517→\n   518→### Advanced Stream Operations\n   519→\n   520→All Phoenix stream operations work seamlessly:\n   521→\n   522→```elixir\n   523→# Add item at specific position\n   524→stream_insert(socket, :items, new_item, at: 0)\n   525→\n   526→# Add multiple items with limits\n   527→stream(socket, :items, new_items, at: -1, limit: -5)\n   528→\n   529→# Reset entire stream\n   530→stream(socket, :items, new_items, reset: true)\n   531→\n   532→# Delete by DOM ID\n   533→stream_delete_by_dom_id(socket, :items, \"item-123\")\n   534→```\n   535→\n   536→The Vue component will automatically receive these updates and maintain its local state throughout all operations.\n   537→\n   538→## Dead Views vs Live Views\n   539→\n   540→Components can be used in both contexts:\n   541→- Live Views: Full reactivity with WebSocket updates\n   542→- Dead Views: Static rendering, no reactivity\n   543→  - `v-socket={@socket}` not required\n   544→  - SSR still works for initial render\n   545→\n   546→## Using ~VUE Sigil\n   547→\n   548→The `~VUE` sigil provides an alternative to the standard LiveView DSL, allowing you to write Vue components directly in your LiveView:\n   549→\n   550→> #### Deprecation Notice {: .warning}\n   551→>\n   552→> The `~V` sigil is deprecated in favor of `~VUE`. It will be removed in future versions.\n   553→\n   554→```elixir\n   555→defmodule MyAppWeb.CounterLive do\n   556→  use MyAppWeb, :live_view\n   557→\n   558→  def render(assigns) do\n   559→    ~VUE\"\"\"\n   560→    <script setup lang=\"ts\">\n   561→    import {ref} from \"vue\"\n   562→    const props = defineProps<{count: number}>()\n   563→    const diff = ref(1)\n   564→    </script>\n   565→\n   566→    <template>\n   567→      Current count: {{ props.count }}\n   568→      <label>Diff: </label>\n   569→      <input v-model.number=\"diff\" type=\"range\" min=\"1\" max=\"10\" />\n   570→\n   571→      <button phx-click=\"inc\" :phx-value-diff=\"diff\">\n   572→        Increase counter by {{ diff }}\n   573→      </button>\n   574→    </template>\n   575→    \"\"\"\n   576→  end\n   577→\n   578→  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do\n   579→    {:ok, assign(socket, count: 0)}\n   580→  end\n   581→\n   582→  def handle_event(\"inc\", %{\"diff\" => diff}, socket) do\n   583→    {:noreply, update(socket, :count, &(&1 + String.to_integer(diff)))}\n   584→  end\n   585→end\n   586→```\n   587→\n   588→The `~VUE` sigil is a powerful macro that compiles the string content into a full-fledged Vue component at compile time. It automatically passes all of the LiveView's `assigns` as props to the component, making it easy to create reactive components.\n   589→\n   590→**When to use the `~VUE` sigil:**\n   591→\n   592→*   **Prototyping:** Quickly build and iterate on new components without creating new files.\n   593→*   **Single-use components:** Ideal for components that are tightly coupled to a specific LiveView and won't be reused.\n   594→*   **Co-location:** Keep server-side and client-side logic for a piece of functionality within a single file.\n   595→\n   596→**When to use `.vue` files instead:**\n   597→\n   598→*   **Reusability:** When you need to use the same component in multiple LiveViews.\n   599→*   **Large components:** For complex components, a dedicated file improves organization and editor support.\n   600→*   **Collaboration:** Separate files are often easier for teams to work on simultaneously.\n   601→\n   602→## Next Steps\n   603→\n   604→Now that you understand the basics, you might want to explore:\n   605→\n   606→- [Forms and Validation](forms.md) for complex forms with server-side validation\n   607→- [Component Reference](component_reference.md) for complete syntax documentation\n   608→- [Configuration](configuration.md) for advanced setup and customization options\n   609→- [Client-Side API](client_api.md) for detailed API reference and advanced patterns\n   610→- [FAQ](faq.md) for common questions and troubleshooting\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"